inish_guard proc~response_snapshot->proc~start_guard interface~z_faddeeva_value z_faddeeva_value proc~zet_in->interface~z_faddeeva_value proc~zetout zetout proc~zet_in->proc~zetout proc~poly_cc poly_cc interface~poly->proc~poly_cc proc~poly_dd poly_dd interface~poly->proc~poly_dd proc~poly_ddv poly_ddv interface~poly->proc~poly_ddv proc~poly_msk_ddv poly_msk_ddv interface~poly->proc~poly_msk_ddv proc~poly_msk_rrv poly_msk_rrv interface~poly->proc~poly_msk_rrv proc~poly_rc poly_rc interface~poly->proc~poly_rc proc~poly_rr poly_rr interface~poly->proc~poly_rr proc~poly_rrv poly_rrv interface~poly->proc~poly_rrv proc~bessim->proc~bessim0 interface~assert assert proc~bessim->interface~assert proc~bessim0->interface~poly proc~bessim1->interface~poly proc~evaluate evaluate proc~calc_chi_adaptive->proc~evaluate proc~fetch_bessel fetch_bessel proc~calc_chi_adaptive->proc~fetch_bessel proc~evaluate~2 evaluate proc~calc_chi_kinetic->proc~evaluate~2 proc~end_arithmetic->proc~finite~2 proc~end_arithmetic->proc~invalid proc~validate->proc~finite~2 proc~zetout->interface~z_faddeeva_value proc~zetout_legacy zetout_legacy proc~zetout->proc~zetout_legacy proc~assert1 assert1 interface~assert->proc~assert1 proc~assert2 assert2 interface~assert->proc~assert2 proc~assert3 assert3 interface~assert->proc~assert3 proc~assert4 assert4 interface~assert->proc~assert4 proc~assert_v assert_v interface~assert->proc~assert_v proc~fetch_bessel->proc~emit_warning proc~fetch_bessel->proc~harmonic_tail_bounds proc~scaled_bessel_array scaled_bessel_array proc~fetch_bessel->proc~scaled_bessel_array proc~select_harmonics select_harmonics proc~fetch_bessel->proc~select_harmonics proc~poly_ddv->proc~poly_dd proc~poly_msk_ddv->proc~poly_ddv proc~poly_msk_rrv->proc~poly_rrv proc~poly_rrv->proc~poly_rr cexp cexp proc~zetout_legacy->cexp float float proc~zetout_legacy->float proc~scaled_bessel_array->proc~harmonic_tail_bounds proc~scaled_bessel_array->proc~select_harmonics proc~select_harmonics->proc~harmonic_tail_bounds Help Graph Key Nodes of different colours represent the following: Graph Key Subroutine Subroutine Function Function Interface Interface Type Bound Procedure Type Bound Procedure Unknown Procedure Type Unknown Procedure Type Program Program This Page's Entity This Page's Entity Solid arrows point from a procedure to one which it calls. Dashed \narrows point from an interface to procedures which implement that interface.\nThis could include the module procedures in a generic interface or the\nimplementation in a submodule of an interface in a parent module. Variables Type Attributes Name Initial integer :: is Index for looping through parametric variations with om_scan . integer :: iroot Index for making many JET-PLUME signatures for each root","tags":"","url":"program/plume.html"},{"title":"  params – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description heating_convention character(len=16) 'corrected' Collisionless heating/energy convention; independent of Z and Bessel modes. z_backend character(len=16) 'faddeeva' Independent Z evaluator: faddeeva (default) or legacy. bessel_mode character(len=16) 'adaptive' bessel_tol real 1.e-10 bessel_nmax integer 512 bessel_nfixed integer 128 bessel_diagnostics logical .false. betap real None Reference Species Parallel Thermal-to-Magnetic Pressure Ratio.\n8 pi n_ref T_ref,par/B&#94;2 kperp real None Wavenumber perpendicular to B0, in the selected input length units.\nrho_ref uses v_tperp,ref/|Omega_ref|; d_ref uses c/omega_p,ref. kpar real None Wavenumber parallel to B0, in the selected input length units. vtp real None normalization character(len=16) 'rho_ref' Input convention: rho_ref (legacy) or d_ref (inertial length). nspec integer None Number of species/components to be included in calculation. nscan integer 0 Number of parameter scans. option integer None Selection for the type of dispersion calculation to be undertaken.\n-1: Calculate disp(om) at a single (omega, gamma)\n 0: Calculate Roots for input plasma parameters.\n 1: Calculate Roots for input plasma parameters or Reads in root value\n    and then scan over plasma parameters, with range and type specified in .in file.\n 2: Calculate Roots for input plasma parameters or Reads in root value\n    and then scan over two-dimensional plasma parameters space,\n    with range and type specified in .in file.\n 3: deprecated\n 4: Make multiple maps of complex frequency space.\n 5: Find roots for parameters along a prescribed path\n    Path is set by solar wind models, with values calculated and\n    output by helper function (in development, the radial scan function.). nroot_max integer None Input specified nroots to follow. use_map logical None Determines method for selecting initial solutions.\nT-> Use map routine to determine solutions in defined regions of complex frequency space.\n    Map parameters determined by &maps list in .in file.\nF-> Read nroot_max initial guesses for complex frequency solutions.\n    Guesses determined by &guess_N lists in .in file. low_n logical .true. Toggle on low-n susceptibility suplementary calculation. collision_type integer 0 Type of collisions for system.\n0: no collisions; standard PLUME\n1: non-conservative krook operator,\n   neutral-charged collisions only. Kn real None Knudson Number for neutral particles over length $\\rho_{ref}$\nKn&#94;{-1} = n_0 \\sigma \\rho_{ref} writeOut logical None Enables or suppressed output to screen. dataName character(len=100) None Data Subdirectory where output is stored. outputName character(len=100) None Common name string for output files. warnings_to_stdout logical .false.","tags":"","url":"namelist/params.html"},{"title":"  fpc – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description vperpmin real None vperpmax real None vparmin real None vparmax real None delv real None vxmin real None vxmax real None vymin real None vymax real None vzmin real None vzmax real None elecdircontribution real None","tags":"","url":"namelist/fpc.html"},{"title":"  species – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description tauS real None Parallel Temperature Ratio. muS real None Mass Ratio. alphS real None Temperature Anisotropy. Qs real None Relative charge ratio. Ds real None Density Ratio. vvS real None Relative Drift, normalized to reference Alfven velocity with .","tags":"","url":"namelist/species.html"},{"title":"  maps – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description loggridw logical None Set log or linear spacing for real frequency axis of the map search. loggridg logical None Set log or linear spacing for imaginary frequency axis of the map search. omi real None Lower bound on real frequency map search axis. omf real None Upper bound on real frequency map search axis. gami real None Lower bound on imaginary frequency map search axis. gamf real None Upper bound on imaginary frequency map search axis. positive_roots logical .false. Consider all solutions (false) or only solutions with positive real frequencies (true). nr integer 128 Number of grid points along real frequency axis\n Now read in by map_read ni integer 128 Number of grid points along imaginary frequency axis\n Now read in by map_read","tags":"","url":"namelist/maps.html"},{"title":"  scan_input – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description scan_type integer None Defines kind of parameter scans. swi real None Initial value of scanned parameter. swf real None Final value of scanned parameter. swlog logical None Linear or Logarithmic scan. scan_style integer None Defines number of components of scan. ns integer None Number of output steps. nres integer None Scan resolution between output steps. heating logical None Controls supplementary heating calculation. eigen logical None Controls supplementary eigenfunction calculation. tensor logical None Controls supplementary output of susceptibility tensor. heating_gamma_ratio_max real None Maximum abs(gamma/omega_r) for corrected heating.","tags":"","url":"namelist/scan_input.html"},{"title":"  guess – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description g_om real None Dummy values for real frequency component of solution. g_gam real None Dummy values for imaginary frequency component of solution.","tags":"","url":"namelist/guess.html"},{"title":"  radial_input – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description nRad integer None Number of points to scan in radial models. nRad_sub integer 1 substepping between nRad points.\nInvoke Linear Interpolation between points modelName character(len=100) None Input file name for radial model radial_heating logical None radial_eigen logical None k_scan integer None Determines wavevector values to include in radial scan\n0: single point in kperp, kpar space\n1: fixed kperp, scan over kpar\n2: fixed kpar,  scan over kperp\n3: fixed theta, scan over k\n4: fixed k, scan over theta\n5: plane scan over (kperp, kpar)\n6: plane scan over (k, theta)","tags":"","url":"namelist/radial_input.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description kperp_1 real None Dummy variable for readin. kpar_1 real None Dummy variable for readin.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description kperp_1 real None Dummy variable for readin. kpar_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. kpar_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~2.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description kperp_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. kperp_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. kpar_1 real None Dummy variable for fixed . nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~3.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description k_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. k_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. theta_1 real None Dummy variable for fixed value (in deg.) nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~4.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description k_1 real None Dummy variable for fixed value. theta_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value (in deg.). theta_2 real None Dummy variable for final value (in deg.). nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~5.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description kperp_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. kperp_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. kpar_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. kpar_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. nK2 integer None Number of output values. kres2 integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log_perp logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_log_par logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~6.html"},{"title":"  k_range – PLUME\n","text":"Variables Name Type Default Description k_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. k_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. theta_1 real None Dummy variable for initial value. theta_2 real None Dummy variable for final value. nK integer None Number of output values. kres integer None Number of steps between output values. ntheta integer None Number of output values. thetares integer None Number of steps between output values. rad_log_k logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_log_theta logical None Logirithmic or linear scan. rad_heat logical None True turns on supplemental heating calculation. rad_eigen logical None True turns on supplemental eigenfunction calculation.","tags":"","url":"namelist/k_range~7.html"},{"title":"Plasma in a Linear Uniform Magnetized Environment – PLUME","text":"User Manual PLUME solves linear electromagnetic dispersion relations in homogeneous,\nnonrelativistic magnetized plasmas, with drifting kinetic bi-Maxwellian and\ncollisionless cold-fluid populations. This manual documents the physical\nconventions, supported calculations, numerical methods, output diagnostics and\nlimitations of the solver. For a first calculation, start with installation and the quick start . Table of Contents Topic Documentation Building and testing Installation First calculation Quick Start Input parameters Input reference Physical scales and units Normalization Kinetic, cold and mixed populations Species and models Root finding, maps and scans Calculation modes Fields, density and velocity perturbations Eigenfunctions and moments Absorption and formal wave energy Heating and wave energy Output files and Python readers Outputs Worked calculations Examples Numerical methods Numerics Restrictions and troubleshooting Limitations Historical numerical choices Reproducibility API, tests and development reports Developer guide Historical all-kinetic FPC JET-PLUME The manual describes current behavior. Development reports document earlier\nimplementation milestones and may contain restrictions subsequently removed. Authors, scientific references and citation PLUME is maintained by Kristopher Klein (kgklein@arizona.edu), Gregory Howes\n(gregory-howes@uiowa.edu), and Collin Brown (collin.crbrown@gmail.com).\nThe kinetic solver descends from Quataert (1998) and Gregory Howes's Fortran adaptation. Its kinetic equations follow Stix, Waves in Plasmas , Chapter 10, equations 66–73. The cold drifting response\nfollows Verscharen & Chandran (2013), Section 2.1 .\nThe selected kinetic heating channels are discussed in the PLUME code paper and Huang et al. (2024) . For publications, cite the PLUME code paper, acknowledge the repository , and record the source revision\nand numerical settings. The archived code-release citation is: @software { PLUME_2025 , author = {{Klein}, K. G. and {Howes}, G. G.} , title = {kgklein/PLUME: Zenodo release} , month = April , year = {2025} , publisher = {Zenodo} , version = {v1.0.1} , doi = {10.5281/zenodo.15215514} , url = {https://doi.org/10.5281/zenodo.15215514} } The archived release predates some current capabilities. Publications using\nPLUME are collected in the NASA ADS and NASA SciX libraries. PLUME uses the BSD 2-Clause license .\nThe bundled Steven G. Johnson Faddeeva implementation has a separate MIT license .","tags":"","url":"page/index.html"},{"title":"Installation and verification – PLUME","text":"Installation and verification Manual home · Next: Quick Start System Requirements Activity Requirements Compile/run the solver GNU Fortran, an ordinary C99 compiler, GNU make, standard Fortran/C runtime libraries and libm Read/write inputs and analyze output w/ Python Python 3, NumPy, Matplotlib Field Particle Correlation analysis w/ Python Python 3, NumPy, Matplotlib Independent reference tests SciPy, NumPy, Matplotlib; Python's unittest is built in Optional high-precision tests mpmath; tests fall back or skip the specific optional references when unavailable Plotting Matplotlib; historical .plt examples require gnuplot Local documentation FORD and its Python dependencies; Graphviz for API graphs The PLUME plasma dispersion function is bundled and compiled into the executable.\nUsers do not need SciPy, libcerf, a C++ runtime, CMake, Python, or a\npackage manager to run the solver .\nPython packages were was used as an external, independent validation of some of the underlying methods. Caveats: GNU Fortran/C 11.4.0 and GNU make 4.3 have been exercised on Linux.\nRecent validation used Python 3.10.12, NumPy 2.2.6, SciPy 1.15.3 and Matplotlib 3.10.8.\nOther platforms/compilers are not certified by the presence of an alternative Makefile rule.\nPreserve the project's promoted-double and strict-IEEE rules,\nas changing precision or optimization can alter legacy regressions and validation. Get the source and compile git clone https://github.com/kgklein/PLUME.git cd PLUME\nmake -j2 The resulting executable is plume.e . To rebuild from source: make clean\nmake -j2 The default Makefile selects its GNU configuration. The existing flags promote\nFortran default real to binary64; several numerical modules are built with\nstrict floating-point semantics. The C99 Faddeeva sources and ABI bridge are\nbuilt automatically. Do not link arbitrary kind/precision variants together.\nThe numerical-method notes explain the precision interface. Run from the repository root mkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in The command takes an input filename ending in .in .\nIts indexed namelist extractor creates a scratch file beside that input,\nso that directory must be writable.\nIt also has a historical 100-character argument buffer, so avoid very\nlong input paths. This will be fixed in future revisions. The input's dataName='example' selects data/example/ relative to the working directory , not relative to the input file. PLUME does not create directories automatically. Files and numerical metadata may be opened even with writeOut=.false. as this flag reduces stdout verbosity and is not a master file-output switch.\nUse a unique outputName and an isolated directory for exploratory runs.\nFiles with matching names are replaced Follow the Quick Start before attempting anything major. Documentation sources and local builds The editable user-manual sources live in docs/ , with the homepage in docs/index.md and images in docs/assets/ . \nThe root README.md is the GitHub project overview, \nand ford_project.md contains the FORD configuration and API landing-page body. \nEdit the Markdown sources, not the generated HTML. Install FORD in a separate environment if desired ( python3 -m pip install ford )\nand provide Graphviz dot for graphs. \nFrom the repository root, build and open the complete local manual with: ./scripts/build_docs.sh\nxdg-open docs_out/page/index.html The builder stages copies in a temporary directory, converts local Markdown\nlinks, copies required assets, and validates the manual before replacing docs_out/ . \nThe previous output is preserved in a uniquely named backup.\nThe same builder runs in documentation CI; building locally does not publish a\nwebsite. See Developer documentation for validation details and remaining FORD limitations.","tags":"","url":"page/INSTALL.html"},{"title":"Quick Start: a small mixed-plasma wave – PLUME","text":"Quick Start: a small mixed-plasma wave Manual home · Installation · Input reference For a first calculation, let's use the mixed_fields_warm_reference.in .\nThis simulates a warm, isotropic proton VDF with cold electrons.\nThe solution is at an oblique wavevector, \ntracking one supplied complex-frequency guess for the solution, \nand follows along ten steps in wavevector space. 1. Build and run From the repository root: make -j2\nmkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in The input directory must be writable for the indexed-namelist scratch file.\nThe supplied example has writeOut=.true. , so it also prints root information.\nThe primary file is: data/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference_kpar_60_90.mode1 The integer filename tags encode scan endpoints.\nRead the numeric columns in the file for details. \nProduced metadata include normalization_mixed_fields_warm_reference.txt and mixed_fields_warm_reference.bessel . \nThe exact normalization metadata details are documented in the output reference . 2. Read the input in physical terms This compact rendering has the same essential state and scan as the existing\nexample; its optional Bessel diagnostics are omitted here for readability: & params normalization = 'd_ref' , betap = . 4 , vtp = . 01 kperp = . 05 , kpar = . 06 nspec = 2 , nscan = 1 , option = 1 , nroot_max = 1 use_map = . false ., writeOut = . true . dataName = 'example' , outputName = 'mixed_fields_warm_reference' / & species_1 tauS = . 4 , muS = 1 , alphS = 1.3 , Qs = 1 , Ds = 1 , vvS = 0 / & species_2 tauS = 0 , muS = 1836 , alphS = 0 , Qs =- 1 , Ds = 1 , vvS = 0 / & guess_1 g_om = . 06 , g_gam =- . 0001 / & scan_input_1 scan_style = 0 , scan_type = 1 , swi = . 06 , swf = . 09 ns = 10 , nres = 2 , swlog = . false . eigen = . true ., heating = . false ., tensor = . false . / Keep each indexed &species_N , &guess_N and &scan_input_N header alone on\nits own line , in lowercase. The Fortran extractor is more restrictive than\nordinary case-insensitive namelist field syntax. Here vtp=.01 means , and kpar / kperp mean , . The warm proton tauS=.4 is its\nAlfvénic parallel temperature and equals reference betap . Electron tauS=0 selects a true cold-fluid response; its anisotropy placeholder is ignored.\nSigned Qs=-1 gives negative electron gyrofrequency. option=1 refines the guess and then continues a branch through serial scans. ns=10 means ten output intervals, eleven written points . nres=2 inserts hidden continuation substeps. \nThe written parallel coordinates are linear steps between .06 and .09; the physical plasma state and perpendicular scale remain fixed.\nFor how to vary other parameters, see input reference The default plasma dispersion function Z backend is Faddeeva, \nwith adaptive Bessel selection, \nand a corrected heating convention. 3. Inspect the first result For the example input, the first row should give approximately The negative imaginary part signifies damping with time, not spatial attenuation.\nThe run should produce eleven rows with finite frequencies, no scan root failed warning, and finite E/B/moments.\nThe code exiting alone does not certify a valid mode or branch. The file has 46 columns: six coordinates/root entries, 12 field entries,\n16 moment entries and 12 species parameters. Columns 1–6 (one-based) are native\nkperp, kpar, betap, vtp, real frequency, imaginary frequency. Columns 7–12 are\nB real/imaginary pairs; 13–18 are E pairs. The remaining blocks are described\nwithout hard-coded species assumptions in the output schema . 4. Read with Python import numpy as np from linfpclib.linfpc import load_plume_sweep path = 'data/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference_kpar_60_90.mode1' d = load_plume_sweep ( path , nspec = 2 , eigen = True , heating = False ) w = d [ 'w' ] + 1 j * d [ 'g' ] print ( w ) assert len ( w ) == 11 and np . isfinite ( w ) . all () print ( 'Ex:' , d [ 'exr' ][ 0 ] + 1 j * d [ 'exi' ][ 0 ]) # 1+0j The Ex=1 convention fixes complex amplitude and phase. E is divided by deltaEx,\nB by the same deltaEx in Gaussian units. \nSee Eigenfunctions and moments before\ncomparing these amplitudes with dimensional observations. 5. Next Steps For all-kinetic absorption and wave energy, run ./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_kinetic.in and inspect its .energy companion, as explained in Heating and wave energy . For a cold physical reference, run ./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_cold_reference.in . For equivalent physical inputs in both normalizations, use the matched pair . For maps or 2D scans, read Calculation modes and use the examples index . If no file appears, check the working directory, dataName , flags and input file.\nFor NaNs or missing eigenfunctions, inspect warnings and diagnostic statuses; troubleshooting helps to distinguish a \"bad root\" from an unavailable\nsupplementary quantity.","tags":"","url":"page/tutorial.html"},{"title":"Input reference – PLUME","text":"Input reference Manual home · Normalization · Modes This reference covers the 89 distinct case-insensitive input names used by PLUME.\nThey are invoked across 14 namelist definitions, \nwhere seven alternative k_range layouts share names.\nThe tables describe the parsers and checks.\nThe source of truth is functions.f90 namelists and vars.f90 initialization. Syntax and required values An input file uses &params ... / , \nthen indexed &species_1 through &species_nspec namelists,\nplus maps/guesses/scans/FPC/radial groups as selected. \nPut each indexed header alone on a lowercase line , \nwithout a trailing comment. \nThe custom indexed extractor is case-sensitive even though scalar field names are not. Ordinary commas or newlines can separate scalar assignments. \nQuote character values and use .true. / .false. for clarity. \nSupply all required species values, including a placeholder alphS for cold species. \nSee the complete working annotated Quick Start . In the below table, “No safe default” means there is no explicit initialization establishing a public value: supply it whenever the group/workflow uses it . \nA default removes the requirement to write that particular value. Common public links: physical parameters , species classification , scan semantics , heating convention , numerical backends , FPC restrictions , output schemas . Global controls: &params This group is required for every run. \nSpecies type and available diagnostics depend on the selected workflow and geometry.\nAll quantities must be interpreted in the selected normalization.\nThe first species is the reference. Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Existing example heating_convention (character(16)) corrected Diagnostic convention; corrected complete real-frequency energy and initialized same-frequency harmonics; legacy original calc_eigen arithmetic. Units: dimensionless label. Values: 'corrected'  or  'legacy'. heating_corrected_cold.in z_backend (character(16)) faddeeva Plasma dispersion Z backend, Z=i sqrt(pi) wofz; signed kpar convention retained. Units: dimensionless label. Values: 'faddeeva'  or  'legacy'. heating_corrected_kinetic.in bessel_mode (character(16)) adaptive Species-specific warm harmonic policy. Units: maximum absolute signed order N, sum -N..N. Values: 'adaptive'  or  'fixed'  or  'fixed128'. adaptive_bessel.in bessel_tol (real) 1e-10 Ordinary and n&#94;2-weighted Bessel tail tolerance, not guaranteed tensor/root error. Units: dimensionless. Values: finite 0 < value < 1. adaptive_bessel.in bessel_nmax (integer) 512 Adaptive maximum harmonic cap; warning and continue on unresolved tail. Units: absolute order. Values: 1..46338. adaptive_bessel.in bessel_nfixed (integer) 128 Fixed maximum absolute harmonic order. Units: absolute order. Values: 1..46338. See group workflow / tests bessel_diagnostics (logical) .false. Enable .bessel cache/cutoff/convergence metadata. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. adaptive_bessel.in betap (real) No safe default 8*pi*nR*Tparallel,R/B0&#94;2. Units: parallel reference beta. Values: positive for warm reference; exactly zero for cold reference. d_ref requires tauS(1)=betap within 1e-8 relative adaptive_bessel.in kperp (real) No safe default Perpendicular wavevector component, k=(kperp,0,kpar). Units: k*rho_R or k*d_R. Values: finite; mixed/cold can be exactly zero. Cold/mixed exact parallel available adaptive_bessel.in kpar (real) No safe default Parallel wavevector component. Units: k*rho_R or k*d_R. Values: finite; nonzero in every wavevector scan and warm susceptibility. adaptive_bessel.in vtp (real) No safe default rho_ref: reference parallel thermal speed/c; d_ref: reference Alfven speed/c. Units: v_tparallel,R/c or vA,R/c. Values: positive. adaptive_bessel.in normalization (character(16)) rho_ref Physical input length/temperature/velocity convention. Units: rho_ref uses perpendicular reference thermal gyroradius; d_ref uses inertial length. Values: 'rho_ref'  or  'd_ref'. adaptive_bessel.in nspec (integer) No safe default Number of populations, in original indexed order. Units: count. Values: must be positive; parser lacks explicit complete bounds check. adaptive_bessel.in nscan (integer) 0 Number of serial scans or 2D coordinate axes. Units: count. Values: nonnegative; 2 for option=2; option=4 one scan. adaptive_bessel.in option (integer) No safe default Executable workflow selector. Units: enumeration. Values:  -1 direct; 0 root map; 1 supplied/map roots + serial scans; 2 grid; 4 repeated maps; 5 radial; 6 gyro FPC; 7 Cartesian FPC. adaptive_bessel.in nroot_max (integer) No safe default Number of supplied guesses / continued branches, not a guaranteed cap on .roots file lines. Units: count. Values: positive; effective storage ceiling numroots=500, not fully input-validated. adaptive_bessel.in use_map (logical) No safe default Choose frequency map search versus supplied guesses. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. adaptive_bessel.in low_n (logical) .true. Select extra n=0,+1,-1 harmonic outputs in historical mode blocks. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. See group workflow / tests collision_type (integer) 0 Collision operator selection. Units: enumeration. Values: 0 collisionless; 1 nonconservative Krook; other values STOP. adaptive_bessel.in Kn (real) 1e+30 Neutral mean free path / selected reference length. Units: Kn_d=Kn_rho*sqrt(beta_R*alpha_R). Values: positive physical mean free path ratio; incomplete finite/positivity validation. See group workflow / tests writeOut (logical) No safe default Verbosity control for screen output, not a prohibition on files. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. adaptive_bessel.in warnings_to_stdout (logical) .false. Copy warnings from .warn to stdout, independently of writeOut . Units: logical. Values: .true. or .false.. See warning output dataName (character(100)) No safe default Subdirectory under data/ for output. Units: path. Values: existing relative data subdirectory. adaptive_bessel.in outputName (character(100)) No safe default Common prefix for mode/maps/metadata outputs. Units: filename component. Values: output basename. adaptive_bessel.in Species: &species_N Supply six fields for every indexed population. tauS=0 selects cold only in d_ref; any positive value remains kinetic. Species ordering is never changed automatically. Name / type Definition with rho_ref Definition with d_ref Application notes Existing example tauS (real) Tparallel,R/Tparallel,s. Units: inverse temperature ratio. Values: >0. 2*Tparallel,s/(mR*vA&#94;2). Units: Alfvenic temperature. Values: >=0; exact zero selects cold. Every species. Exact zero is model selector only in d_ref; negative invalid; no small-temperature threshold. adaptive_bessel.in muS (real) Reference/species mass ratio mR/ms. Units: dimensionless. Values: positive. Reference/species mass ratio mR/ms. Units: dimensionless. Values: positive. Every species. Physical reference value should be1; parser warns only for mu1>1+1e-7, not symmetric/terminal. adaptive_bessel.in alphS (real) Species perpendicular/parallel temperature ratio. Units: dimensionless. Values: >0. Species perpendicular/parallel temperature ratio. Units: dimensionless. Values: warm >0; cold ignored. Every species; cold placeholder irrelevant. Physical alpha_s distinct from auxiliary alpha*=1; rho_R uses alpha_R. adaptive_bessel.in Qs (real) Reference/species charge ratio qR/qs. Units: dimensionless. Values: nonzero, signed. Reference/species charge ratio qR/qs. Units: dimensionless. Values: nonzero, signed. Every species. Gyrofrequency mu/Q signed; plasma prefactor uses Q&#94;2. No neutrality hard constraint. adaptive_bessel.in Ds (real) Species/reference density ratio ns/nR. Units: dimensionless. Values: nonnegative physical density; warm-only validation incomplete. Species/reference density ratio ns/nR. Units: dimensionless. Values: nonnegative physical density; cold references/cold kernels enforce; warm-only validation incomplete. Every species. Zero density has unavailable relative-density moments; not physically defined zero perturbation. adaptive_bessel.in vvS (real) Equilibrium Us/vA,R along B0. Units: Alfven velocity. Values: finite signed parallel drift. Equilibrium Us/vA,R along B0. Units: Alfven velocity. Values: finite signed parallel drift. Every species, including species1 with warning. No forced zero-current frame or automatic charge/current balancing. adaptive_bessel.in Supplied frequencies: &guess_N Provide one group per requested guess when use_map=.false. in root workflows, and for direct option=-1. Only the first frequency is directly evaluated by test_disp. Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example g_om (real) No safe default Real part of supplied frequency. Units: Omega_R. Values: finite supplied frequency guess. &guess_j, use_map=false; direct option-1 uses first. Secant roots are refined except direct evaluation. See group workflow / tests g_gam (real) No safe default Imaginary part of supplied frequency; growth positive. Units: Omega_R. Values: finite, signed. &guess_j. Strongly damped/growing heating can be qualified/unavailable. See group workflow / tests Frequency maps: &maps Required for option=0/4 or any map-initialized workflow. Use both log flags explicitly; linear maps are recommended. These flags concern the frequency plane, not wavevector scan interpolation. Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example loggridw (logical) No safe default Select the historical inconsistent logarithmic real-frequency map formula; linear recommended. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. &maps, option0/maps. See Modes for the exact origin/increment equations; bounds are not physical endpoints when true. See group workflow / tests loggridg (logical) No safe default Select the historical inconsistent logarithmic imaginary-frequency map formula; linear recommended. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. &maps. Imaginary signs discarded and all resulting nodes negative; see Modes. See group workflow / tests omi (real) No safe default Initial real-frequency coordinate for a linear map; historical log origin differs. Units: Omega_R. Values: finite; >0 if log. &maps. Linear endpoints work; log origin currently exponentiates omi rather than log10(omi). See group workflow / tests omf (real) No safe default Final real-frequency coordinate for a linear map; historical log endpoint differs. Units: Omega_R. Values: finite; >0 if log. &maps. Bounds ordering/grid positivity not comprehensively validated. See group workflow / tests gami (real) No safe default Initial signed imaginary-frequency coordinate for a linear map; historical log origin differs. Units: Omega_R, positive growth. Values: finite; nonzero magnitude if log. &maps. Log mode always negative; intended bounds currently not reproduced. See group workflow / tests gamf (real) No safe default Final signed imaginary-frequency coordinate for a linear map; historical log endpoint differs. Units: Omega_R. Values: finite; nonzero magnitude if log. &maps. Same log-origin issue as gami. See group workflow / tests positive_roots (logical) .false. Select candidates with real frequency > -1e-10 after map file writing. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Map-selected continuation roots. Does not mean positive growth; .roots written before final filter; hard gamma<1 filter also exists. See group workflow / tests nr (integer) 128 Real-frequency map intervals, generating nr+1 nodes. Units: interval count. Values: positive intended, not fully validated. &maps. Default128 ->129 nodes; grid has (nr+1)*(ni+1) rows. See group workflow / tests ni (integer) 128 Imaginary-frequency map intervals, generating ni+1 nodes. Units: interval count. Values: positive intended, not fully validated. &maps. Default128 ->129 nodes. See group workflow / tests Scans: &scan_input_N Supply nscan indexed groups for serial scans or exactly two axes for option=2. Defaults for diagnostics reset false for each group; range/style/count controls have no safe default. See scan mappings for the overloaded swi/swf meanings. Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example scan_type (integer) No safe default Select coordinate inside scan style. Units: enumeration. Values: style-1:0 vector,1 angle,2 magnitude; style0:0 kp,1 kz,2 beta,3 vtp; species:0 tau,1 mu,2 alpha,3 Q,4 D,5 U. &scan_input_j. Vector swi/swf are final kp/kz; angle uses swi as terminal angle; magnitude uses swf; not generic two endpoints. cold_fields_parallel.in swi (real) No safe default Scan range_i; compound vector/angle overload. Units: native coordinate; angle degrees. Values: coordinate-dependent valid initial/target value. &scan_input_j. For style-1 vector: target kperp; angle: target theta; initial physical point comes from current plasma state. cold_fields_parallel.in swf (real) No safe default Scan range_f; compound vector/magnitude overload. Units: native coordinate; angle degrees. Values: coordinate-dependent valid final/target value. &scan_input_j. For style-1 vector: target kpar; magnitude: terminal signed  or k or . cold_fields_parallel.in swlog (logical) No safe default Logarithmic or linear continuation coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Scans. Log wavevector coordinates require positive endpoints; use linear for negative kpar. cold_fields_parallel.in scan_style (integer) No safe default Select family of scan coordinate. Units: enumeration. Values: -1 compound k;0 globals;1..nspec individual species. Options1/2/4. Corrected parameter heating: collisionless all-kinetic rho_ref, options1/2. Mixed/cold allow wavevector scans only. cold_fields_parallel.in ns (integer) No safe default Number of output intervals; normally ns+1 output points. Units: count. Values: positive for wavevector and corrected rho_ref parameter scans. Scans. Internal steps ns*nres; hidden substeps can fail without a written point. cold_fields_parallel.in nres (integer) No safe default Substeps per output interval. Units: count. Values: positive for wavevector and corrected rho_ref parameter scans. Scans. Writes when mod(internal_index,nres)=0; companion point is internal index. cold_fields_parallel.in heating (logical) .false. Request species absorption and channels; corrected also companion energy. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Serial/2D, grid scan_input_2. Corrected collisionless wavevector scans and all-kinetic rho_ref parameter scans; second-axis flag in 2D. Maps reject corrected and warn/ignore legacy. Cold kinetic slots NaN, warm totals/channels retained. cold_fields_parallel.in heating_gamma_ratio_max (real) 0.05 Maximum abs(gamma/omega_r) for corrected heating applicability. Units: dimensionless fraction; 0.05 means 5%. Values: finite, nonnegative; zero permits only zero damping/growth under this check. Resets to default for each &scan_input_N. Serial scans use their own setting; option=2 uses scan_input_2. Applicability bit 1 is set only when the ratio strictly exceeds this limit; equality passes. Relaxing it enables normalized Ps/channels only if all other checks pass; the independent energy-balance check is unchanged. Ignored by legacy heating. Set heating_gamma_ratio_max=0.05 in &scan_input_N. eigen (logical) .false. Request E/B and existing kinetic/cold density/velocity eigenfunctions. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Serial/2D; grid uses scan_input_2. Serial uses this scan; 2D uses second axis. Map request warns and is ignored. Ex=1 field and per-species moment failures are separate. cold_fields_parallel.in tensor (logical) .false. Request six independent complex susceptibility elements per species. Units: ordinary chi. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Serial/2D output; grid requires BOTH tensor flags. Serial scan flag; 2D requires both axes true. Map requests warn and are ignored. make_scan(..., tensor=False) exposes the independent option. cold_fields_parallel.in FPC: &fpc Used by options 6/7 only, in all-kinetic configurations. Grids are historical species-parallel-thermal-speed coordinates; all component bounds and delv should be integer-compatible. FPC normalization and numerical moment closure have caveats; see the FPC appendix . Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example vperpmin (real) No safe default Velocity-grid minimum for vperp. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vperpmax (real) No safe default Velocity-grid maximum for vperp. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vparmin (real) No safe default Velocity-grid minimum for vpar. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vparmax (real) No safe default Velocity-grid maximum for vpar. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests delv (real) No safe default FPC velocity-grid spacing. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: positive. FPC6/7. No comprehensive grid validation; zero spacing can cause invalid arithmetic. See group workflow / tests vxmin (real) No safe default Velocity-grid minimum for vx. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vxmax (real) No safe default Velocity-grid maximum for vx. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vymin (real) No safe default Velocity-grid minimum for vy. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vymax (real) No safe default Velocity-grid maximum for vy. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vzmin (real) No safe default Velocity-grid minimum for vz. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests vzmax (real) No safe default Velocity-grid maximum for vz. Units: species parallel thermal-speed units in historical FPC grid. Values: finite ordered velocity bounds. FPC option6 gyro or7 Cartesian. All species kinetic; supply bounds explicitly; cold-containing FPC rejected. Header uses solver thermal values even for d_ref input. See group workflow / tests elecdircontribution (real) 0.0 Debug selection of electric forcing components for FPC. Units: selector. Values: 1 Ex only,2 Ey only,3 Ez only;0/other unchanged. FPC6/7. Changes a debug diagnostic forcing, not dispersion. Not new mixed FPC support. See group workflow / tests Experimental radial controls The following &radial_input and selected &k_range fields are retained for option=5, not promoted to general mixed-plasma support. All kinetic only; corrected radial heating is rejected. No small standalone public radial model is currently validated as a primary example. &radial_input Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example nRad (integer) No safe default Number of radial model rows. Units: count. Values: positive required model-row count. Experimental option5. Requires modelName0.rad and one species table per population. Experimental; no primary input nRad_sub (integer) 1 Radial interpolation substeps. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5. Default 1; interpolate in selected input variables, not generally invariant between normalizations. Experimental; no primary input modelName (character(100)) No safe default Prefix of <modelName>0.rad globals and <modelName>s.rad species tables. Units: path. Values: valid input model basename/path. Option5. Global rows radius,beta,vtp; species rows radius,tau,mu,alpha,Q,D,U. Experimental; no primary input radial_heating (logical) No safe default Request historical radial heating. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input radial_eigen (logical) No safe default Request radial fields/moments. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input k_scan (integer) No safe default Radial wavevector selection:0 fixed,1 kz,2 kp,3 magnitude,4 angle,5 kp/kz plane,6 angle/magnitude plane. Units: enumeration. Values: 0..6. Option5. Selects different &k_range namelist layouts; cold-containing radial unsupported. Experimental; no primary input &k_range (choose the layout matching k_scan) These controls belong only to the experimental radial pathway, not ordinary scan_input_N . The layout table below specifies which names may coexist. Angles are degrees; wavevector coordinates use the selected native length. Name / type Default Definition, units and allowed values Applicability, dependencies and actual validation Existing example kperp_1 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input kpar_1 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input kpar_2 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input nK (integer) No safe default Radial k_range output interval count for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input kres (integer) No safe default Radial k_range resolution substeps for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input rad_log (logical) No safe default Choose logarithmic interpolation for the named radial wavevector coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input rad_heat (logical) No safe default Request historical radial heating. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input rad_eigen (logical) No safe default Request radial fields/moments. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input kperp_2 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input k_1 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input k_2 (real) No safe default Initial/final wavevector coordinate in radial k_range layout. Units: native selected k-length convention. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input theta_1 (real) No safe default Initial/final angle in radial k_range layout. Units: degrees. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input theta_2 (real) No safe default Initial/final angle in radial k_range layout. Units: degrees. Values: finite; positive if logarithmic. Option5; k_scan selects active fields. Suffix1 initial/fixed,2 terminal; other fields may be absent for that k_scan layout. Experimental; no primary input nk2 (integer) No safe default Radial k_range output interval count for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input kres2 (integer) No safe default Radial k_range resolution substeps for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input rad_log_perp (logical) No safe default Choose logarithmic interpolation for the named radial wavevector coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input rad_log_par (logical) No safe default Choose logarithmic interpolation for the named radial wavevector coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input ntheta (integer) No safe default Radial k_range output interval count for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input thetares (integer) No safe default Radial k_range resolution substeps for its designated coordinate. Units: count. Values: positive intended. Option5, layout depends on k_scan. Defaults not assigned; nk/nk2 and kres/kres2 for Cartesian axes, ntheta/thetares for angle; model interpolation already separate. Experimental; no primary input rad_log_k (logical) No safe default Choose logarithmic interpolation for the named radial wavevector coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input rad_log_theta (logical) No safe default Choose logarithmic interpolation for the named radial wavevector coordinate. Units: logical. Values: .true.  or  .false.. Experimental radial option5. Corrected radial_heating rejected; use legacy all-kinetic; k_range rad_heat/rad_eigen are read but are not assigned to scan diagnostic fields (global radial flags control output). Experimental; no primary input k_scan Required k_range layout 0, fixed vector kperp_1, kpar_1 1, fixed kperp / varying kpar kperp_1, kpar_1, kpar_2, nK, kres, rad_log, rad_heat, rad_eigen 2, fixed kpar / varying kperp kperp_1, kperp_2, kpar_1, nK, kres, rad_log, rad_heat, rad_eigen 3, fixed angle / varying magnitude k_1, k_2, theta_1, nK, kres, rad_log, rad_heat, rad_eigen 4, fixed magnitude / varying angle k_1, theta_1, theta_2, nK, kres, rad_log, rad_heat, rad_eigen 5, Cartesian plane kperp_1, kperp_2, kpar_1, kpar_2, nk, kres, nk2, kres2, rad_log_perp, rad_log_par, rad_heat, rad_eigen 6, polar plane k_1, k_2, theta_1, theta_2, nk, kres, ntheta, thetares, rad_log_k, rad_log_theta, rad_heat, rad_eigen nK and nk are the same case-insensitive parameter. Model tables contain\nradius in the first column, then beta/vtp in modelName0.rad or the six species\nparameters in modelNameN.rad. nRad sets rows, nRad_sub interpolation substeps.\nradial_eigen/radial_heating concern radial continuation; rad_eigen/rad_heat are\nstored on the auxiliary wavevector-range scan. This historical two-level\ninterface has no primary curated example and should not be generalized without\nseparate validation. The existing synthetic radial test is the developer reference. Interactions and validation gaps Cold-containing inputs require d_ref, collision_type=0, no FPC, and only\n  supported wavevector scans. Cold references require betap=0; warm d_ref\n  references require tauS_1=betap within the implemented relative comparison. Warm-reference adaptation needs positive reference beta, vtp and physical\n  anisotropy. Cold anisotropy is ignored. Modern selectors and Bessel count/tolerance bounds are checked explicitly.\n  Many old finite/count/storage/bound/Kn/FPC-grid checks remain incomplete. Self mass/charge/density ratios are a user consistency responsibility; the old\n  mass-ratio warning is nonterminal and not a comprehensive reference check.\n  Charge neutrality, zero current and reference drift are warning-only, not\n  imposed equilibrium constraints. All-kinetic kperp=0 warns but remains unsupported. Every wavevector scan excludes\n  kpar=0, including hidden points. Do not replace it with an arbitrary small floor. This will be updated in a future release Map eigen/tensor and legacy heating requests warn and remain ignored;\n  corrected map heating is rejected. \n  See Modes rather than assuming\n  that a flag implies a diagnostic file exists. Python wrapper contract linfpclib.linfpc.plume_input supports the scalar params/species/maps/scan/guess/\nfpc groups above. It preserves low_n, both log flags, nr/ni, tensor and all\nCartesian FPC bounds on read/write roundtrips. writeOut is independent of use_map , and positive_roots is independent of loggridw . set_maps(loggridw, omi, omf, gami, gamf, positive_roots,\nloggridg=False, nr=128, ni=128) retains its historical first six arguments. make_scan(..., tensor=False) adds an optional tensor flag. Logical values\nin wrapper dictionaries use canonical Fortran strings. Quoted paths, comments,\nD exponents and scalar logical variants are supported. load_from_file replaces prior indexed state, preserves an explicit dataName,\nand rejects unknown fields/unsupported groups or array/expression syntax instead\nof silently dropping controls. Assign obj.dataname after loading to redirect\noutput. write_input still takes an explicit outputname. Radial/k_range groups\nare not supported by this wrapper. It is not a general Fortran namelist parser.\nExamples and executable reader code are on Output .","tags":"","url":"page/input.html"},{"title":"Normalization and physical units – PLUME","text":"Normalization and physical units Manual home · Input reference · Species models Here, we define the temporal and spatial scales used in PLUME.\nFor brevity, temperatures are in energy units (Boltzmann's constant is absorbed into T).\nElectromagnetic formulas are in Gaussian units. The equilibrium is homogeneous, with\nmean magnetic field and\nwavevector . \nTime dependence is given by .\nThe solution grows when . Reference scales PLUME always treats species_1 as reference R.\nThis species has a positive reference gyrofrequency scale (a positive reference charge and B0), \nwith the other speices retaining signed charge ratios for other populations. For completeness, we define Here c is the speed of light, and , , and set \nthe reference mass, charge, and number density. \nFor the thermal reference define The gyroradius uses the perpendicular thermal speed, while plasma beta uses the parallel reference temperature.\nThis yields and .\nPLUME's Alfvén speed uses reference density/mass, not the mixture's total mass density. Do not substitute a total-mixture Alfvén speed without converting all normalized inputs. Inputs in the two conventions The normalizations are selected using normalization='rho_ref' (default) or 'd_ref' in &params .\nLet , , , and , with j parallel or perpendicular.\nFor a physical population s, use , (signed), , , and . Input rho_ref d_ref betap Reference parallel beta, beta_R Same; zero for a cold reference kperp , kpar , , vtp tauS muS Same Qs , signed Same Ds Same alphS Same for warm species; ignored for cold vvS Same; never thermal-speed-normalized g_om , g_gam , Same Kn Neutral mean free path/ Neutral mean free path/ We use as a symbol for the temperature definition d_ref tauS in this manual\nto avoid confusing a physical temperature with the inverse ratio in rho_ref . Both use the same Fortran input name. Self ratios for the reference population are mu_1 = Q_1 = D_1 =1.\nThe solver does not enforce self-ratio or reference-density consistency;\nunusual component decompositions require a clearly specified reference density. There is no automatic renormalization. For d_ref the physical thermal and species frequency relations are The Doppler-shifted frequency is .\nThe gyrofrequency is signed; the plasma-frequency coefficient uses Q_s squared.\nThese definitions apply to kinetic and cold species. theta_s=0 means cold fluid,\nnot a small positive kinetic temperature. Converting equivalent physical states When and , , , , , and are unchanged.\nMatching collision frequencies additionally requires .\nThe existing derived rates are These rates belong only to the restricted all-kinetic Krook implementation, which is a work in progress. Worked equivalent inputs The unchanged normalization_rho_ref.in and normalization_d_ref.in represent a proton/electron system with beta_R=.4, alpha_R=1.5,\na=1e-4, alpha_e=1, u_e=.02, and theta_e=.2. Quantity rho_ref input d_ref input Initial kperp .03872983346207417 .05 Initial kpar .046475800154489 .06 vtp .00006324555320336759 .0001 Proton tauS 1 .4 Electron tauS 2 .2 The length ratio is sqrt(.4*1.5)=sqrt(.6).\nRun both from the repository root: mkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/normalization_rho_ref.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/normalization_d_ref.in Both first mode rows give, at printed precision, . Show that these two solutions match. Their native wavevector and vtp columns differ by the factors above.\nConvert those columns and then plot overlaying curves. Scan paths Caveat: One of the useful aspects of PLUME is the ability to serially or in parallel\nscan dispersion relation solutions over wavevector and plasma parameters.\nGenerally, the user can select any sets of parameters for these scans.\nThe exception is for a beta_R and theta_s d_ref scans.\nBy definition for the d_ref normalization, changing beta_R also updates theta_1.\nA reference theta_1 scan updates beta_R in return.\nVarying both independently in a 2D scan is rejected.\nA beta-only rho_ref scan traces different a different physical system than that in d_ref .\nConvert every point, including endpoint and interpolation rules, before making a physical comparison. Cold references and the auxiliary thermal scale Cold-containing calculations require selecting the d_ref normalization.\nFor a cold reference use betap=0 and species_1 tauS=0 .\nAll species may be cold, or some may be kinetic.\nNo physical reference thermal speed or gyroradius exists in that limit,\nbut , and remain finite. For warm species with a cold reference,\nthe existing kinetic algebra uses an internal positive scale selected from the first warm species' theta.\nIt constructs auxiliary beta ,\nthermal velocity over c, ,\nwavevector ),\ninverse temperature ,\nand normalization anisotropy .\nThe physical is unchanged.\nThis is simply a coordinate transformation.\nFully cold calculations bypass this thermal conversion entirely. There is no public /auxiliary-temperature namelist control.\nReordering warm non-reference populations can change the internally chosen scale,\nbut results should remain physically equivalent within the documented numerical conditioning. Diagnostic units Let , and .\nWe choose the gauge to fix the shared phase and amplitude eigenvalue convention.\nThis lets us write: Double check velocity perturbation normalization in d_ref units. E and B are Gaussian field ratios.\nSpecies velocity fluctuations are not v_A-normalized\nor thermal-speed-normalized.\nDensity uses each species' own equilibrium .\nThe physical moments follow by multiplying by and by .\nAll amplitudes are generally complex.\nFor drifting populations the current is ,\nnot just . Heating uses dimensionless Formal energy is where H has separate vacuum electric, magnetic and species terms.\nTheir full definitions and applicability live in Heating and wave energy .\nFPC grids and header variables retain historical thermal units;\nsee the FPC appendix .","tags":"","url":"page/normalization.html"},{"title":"Plasma Models – PLUME","text":"Plasma species, models and capabilities Manual home · Normalization · Modes and scans PLUME supports bi-Maxwellian and cold populations in mixtures,\nsubject to the restrictions below.\nIf you use one or more cold populations, you must use the d_ref normalization. Kinetic populations We define a warm population as a drifting gyrotropic bi-Maxwellian\nwith number density , \nparallel drift and \nthermal speeds , . \nIts equilibrium distribution is The magnetic field and drift are oriented along z. \nKinetic anisotropy . \nIn d_ref , = tauS >0 sets the population to be warm, \nwhile tauS=0 sets the population to be cold. In rho_ref tauS is the reference-to-species temperature ratio and must be positive. No threshold switches a sufficiently narrow Maxwellian to cold fluid. Multiple components of the same particle species may be separated into individual componets, with each population having separate temperatures,\ndensities and drifts. Cold-fluid populations Under d_ref , setting tauS=0 selects a cold fluid model. Negative tauS is invalid. A cold population has zero thermal pressure, \nand thus alphS is physically undefined and ignored by its response. \nSupply it with 1 as a  placeholder. Cold species retain finite inertia, signed cyclotron frequency, density,\ncharge and parallel drift. \nTheir response includes oblique drift coupling, not just the stationary Stix transverse terms. \nThe response supports complex frequencies, fully cold systems and a cold reference population. Cold populations have fluid density/velocity eigenfunctions when the Ex=1 field\nsolution and response are valid. Kinetic heating, kinetic harmonic heating and FPC are unavailable for cold species, and report NaN.\nTheir reversible response does, however, contribute to formal wave energy. Mixtures and species ordering PLUME stores ordinary susceptibility separately for each population.\nThe dielectric response is , \nadding the vacuum identity and forming the electromagnetic wave matrix. \nThe full plasma determines the common field polarization, \nwith moments and kinetic absorption use that field with the appropriate per-species response. \nSee Eigenfunctions and Heating for the full expressions. Species_1 remains the physical reference even if cold. \nCold-reference/warm mixtures use the internal auxiliary scale described in Normalization ,\nso there is no need to reorder species merely to obtain a warm reference. Interleaved warm/cold species and several warm populations are supported, subject to the capability below. Capability matrix A list of current and future planned capabilities. S = supported in the current release; P = partially supported with conditions; U = unsupported; NV = not fully validated. All cold columns require normalization='d_ref' and collision_type=0 . \nThe fully kinetic solutions allow for either normalization. Workflow/geometry Kinetic only Fully cold Mixed, warm reference Mixed, cold reference Oblique direct evaluation / supplied roots S S S S Frequency-map root search S S S S Serial / 2D wavevector scans S S S S Repeated frequency maps P P P P Exact parallel kperp=0 U S S U Exact perpendicular kpar=0 single wavevector U P: Ex=1 gauge may fail U U Any wavevector scan containing kpar=0 U U U U Plasma-parameter scans S: corrected collisionless rho_ref ; historical restrictions otherwise U U U Radial scans P: legacy restrictions U U U Finite-rate Krook response P / NV off-diagonal accuracy U U U General collision operator U U U U For scans, even an intermediate step at kpar=0 is prohibited. \nFully cold perpendicular single-point O/X dispersion does not imply perpendicular scans.\nParallel longitudinal or perpendicular ordinary modes may have Ex=0 and thus\nno output eigenfunction in PLUME's chosen gauge. Diagnostic Kinetic only Fully cold Either mixed reference E/B polarization S for valid supported Ex=1 modes S subject to gauge/poles S subject to gauge/poles Species U and density S S fluid moments S kinetic/fluid per population Total species kinetic absorption + seven channels Corrected collisionless; legacy optional NaN Corrected warm species only; cold NaN Complete formal wave energy S with real-frequency weak-mode qualifications S with same qualifications S including all physical species Corrected finite-rate heating U U U Gyro/Cartesian FPC P: historical all-kinetic pathway U U, including warm members General FPC energy/moment closure NV U U Equilibrium checks are warnings With the symbols defined in Normalization , charge and current\nimbalance are proportional to and , respectively.\nInput validation warns at absolute magnitude at least 1e-6. \nA drifting reference also produces a warning. \nNone of these checks changes density, drift, species order or reference frame, and neither charge neutrality nor zero current is mandatory.\nSome nonstandard homogeneous equilibria require external charge, currents or\nforces to maintain the assumed background. \nUsers must make that equilibrium interpretation explicit. Existing examples Run from the repository root after mkdir -p data/example : Composition Command Inspect Kinetic proton/electron ./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_kinetic.in Mode E/B/moments; kinetic Ps; .energy Warm proton / cold electron ./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in Three finite field/moment rows Cold proton / warm electron ./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_cold_reference.in Same units, beta_R=0, no fictitious reference temperature Fully cold ./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_cold.in Fluid moments, finite formal energy, NaN kinetic heat Multiple interleaved populations ./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_moments_multispecies.in All species in original order, signed drift currents Examples and expected results lists additional input files for users to explore.","tags":"","url":"page/models.html"},{"title":"Calculation modes, maps and continuation – PLUME","text":"Calculation modes, maps and continuation Manual home · Input reference · Output schemas The executable reads one input file, builds species responses, and follows\nthe calculation dictated by option . \nThe same production dispersion evaluator and secant root method serve\nkinetic, cold and mixed systems. \nNote that the cold and kinetic geometry restrictions differ, so consult the capability matrix before requesting diagnostics. Create the input's data/<dataName> directory and use distinct output names. Workflow selector option Operation Root initialization Supplementary output -1 Evaluate at supplied complex frequency, without refining it guess_N Text diagnostics and corrected .energy where valid 0 Single-wavevector frequency map and root search maps .map and .roots; no scan eigen/heating/tensor blocks 1 Supplied roots or map initialization, then serial scans use_map chooses maps or guess_N chooses initial roots .modeN; eigen/heating/tensor according to each scan 2 Two-coordinate grid, nscan=2 use_map chooses maps or guess_N chooses initial roots .modeN grid blocks; special flag rules below 3 Deprecated historical hard-wired calculation none Stops; not a supported workflow 4 Repeat frequency maps along one scan, nscan=1 maps at each point Tagged .map/.roots files only 5 Experimental 'radial-model' continuation maps or guess_N Historical radial files; all kinetic only 6 Gyrotropic FPC maps or guess_N Gyro velocity-grid files; all kinetic only 7 Cartesian FPC maps or guess_N Projection/distribution/debug files; all kinetic only Supplied guesses and direct evaluation Provide guess_1 through guess_nroot_max with g_om=omega_r/Omega_R and g_gam=gamma/Omega_R . \nFor root refinement, two nearby points are constructed\nfrom the guess and passed to the existing complex secant method. \nThese are not guaranteed brackets. \nSeveral guesses can converge to the same branch or fail.\nA negative gamma is a damped mode under exp(-i omega t). Existing single-wavevector examples: mkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/cold_proton_electron.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_cold_protons_warm_electrons.in For option =-1, test_disp evaluates only the first supplied frequency as-is and prints the determinant and default diagnostics. The raw API determinant is . \nDirect stdout for a warm reference historically displays \ndet(Lambda)*solver_vtp&#94;6, while a cold reference displays\nthe raw determinant. Numerical methods defines the matrices and scaling. \nNever compare these differently scaled numbers as if they were the same residual. Frequency-map search A map samples complex frequency grid points at fixed plasma and wavevector . \nLocal minima of determinant magnitude serve as the seeds for secant refinement. \nSuccessful candidates are deduplicated using the historical absolute frequency gap 1e-5.\nThe internal candidate storage is 500, not a user-selectable input.\nA minimum is not necessarily a physical zero, and a map cannot guarantee that\nall branches were found. \nMap resolution and bounds require carefuly user selection, some physics knowledge, and/or a convergence study. The workflow distinguishes: Grid points: every sampled determinant, written to .map, including invalid points as NaN on guarded paths. Refined candidates: roots found and deduplicated before final selection,\n   written to .roots. These are not grid frequencies. Selected roots: subsequent filters include and, when positive_roots =true, . Continued branch slots: scans follow nroot_max slots using the existing\n   continuation. They do not continue every .roots row. nroot_max is the supplied-guess/continuation count, not a cap on lines in .roots. \nThe file is written before the later map filters. \nThe optional internal sorting block is disabled by default, so do not assume branches are automatically ranked by least damping. For incomplete searches, inspect candidates and initialize a\nbounded supplied-guess run deliberately. The existing example_map_par.in is a secondary map lesson, not the introductory run: ./plume.e inputs/example/example_map_par.in It writes data/example/dispersion_map_par.map and .roots (and any scans\nrequested by the file). Use the matching input to interpret units. The audit\nfound more .roots rows than nroot_max here; this is expected from writer order. Linear versus historical logarithmic maps For linear flags, i=0..nr and j=0..ni give All coordinates are . nr and ni count intervals, meaning there are grid points. \nSigned imaginary endpoints are ordinary linear bounds. Use linear map grids for physical endpoint control. Logarithmic\nmaps have an unresolved implementation inconsistency, intentionally not changed\nby the documentation or reader corrections. If the respective log flag is true,\nthe actual code instead evaluates The origins are exponent-like, while increments use physical-bound ratios.\nNeither an exponent-bound convention nor a physical-endpoint convention\nexplains both. Real logarithmic endpoints must be positive and imaginary\nmagnitudes nonzero for these logarithms to exist; all imaginary input signs are\ndiscarded and the result is negative. For omi=.1, omf=1 the range is approximately\n1.258925..12.58925; for gami=-1e-4, gamf=-1e-2 it is -1.000230..-100.0230. Serial wavevector scans Set option =1 and give nscan indexed scan_input_N groups. \nAfter initialization, they run serially , \npassing the last state into the next scan. These scans are not independent resets. Match the initial input wavevector and supplied root to the\nfirst scan point, particularly for the unchanged kinetic-only initialization. For an ordinary scan, M= ns * nres continuation intervals are traversed. \nThe code writes the endpoints and every nres -th point, normally ns +1 rows per branch.\nHidden substeps help continuation but do not add rows. \nPositive logarithmic wavevector scans interpolate log10 of coordinates consistently. scan_style scan_type Meaning of swi / swf 0 0 or 1 Initial/final native kperp or kpar -1 0 Target vector ( swi =kperp_final, swf =kpar_final); start is the current wavevector -1 1 Target angle in degrees in swi ; swf unused; magnitude fixed -1 2 Target signed magnitude in swf ; swi unused; angle fixed 0 2 or 3 Historical all-kinetic beta or vtp scan s=1..nspec 0..5 Historical population-s tau , mu , alpha , Q , D or drift scan The compound angle is , with magnitude , not a new quadrant-independent atan2 convention. \nNegative parallel propagation is supported for nonzero points. \nLogarithmic scanned coordinates must be positive, so use linear traversal for negative kpar. Supported mixed/cold scans change wavevectors only. They keep temperatures,\nreference normalization and drifts fixed. kpar=0 is rejected even at hidden\npoints; there is no small-kpar substitution. Will work on getting paramters scans for mixed and colds cases working as future step. ./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_scan_warm_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/fully_cold_scan.in These write branch .modeN files. Two-dimensional wavevector grids Use option =2, nscan =2. \nSupported coordinate pairs are distinct Cartesian\nkperp/kpar axes, or ordered polar axes (scan_input_1 angle, scan_input_2 magnitude).\nThe first scan is outer and the second is inner. Each coordinate uses its own\nns/nres; output has blank separators between outer blocks. The existing large example_guess_double.in provides an example,\nsee the examples index for more. Eigenfunctions and heating in the grid use scan_input_2 switches. \nTensor output requires tensor=.true. in both groups. \nThe code preserves original species order and native coordinates. Root failure and continuation Successful points reuse the existing last-root continuation strategy. \nGuarded scan refinement checks finite roots/tensors, status and a determinant scaled by the maximum wave-matrix element. \nA failed written point has NaN root/diagnostic slots, with parameters retained; a failed hidden point warns without adding a row. \nThe continuation seed remains the last valid one. Near crossings, poles, strong damping or genuine branch termination, forward and reverse traversal can choose\ndifferent branches. \nCheck residuals, polarization and isolated single-wavevector\nrefinements; reduce step size deliberately rather than assuming file continuity\nproves branch identity. Repeated maps and historical parameter/radial workflows Option =4 remaps at each scanned point rather than continuing a root. \nIt accepts only one scan. \nEigen/tensor flags and legacy heating are ignored with warnings;\ncorrected map heating is rejected. \nFiles remain determinant maps and candidate\nroots, without supplementary blocks. \nUse a separately initialized supported\nroot/scan workflow for diagnostics. No heating is added to maps. Collisionless, all-kinetic rho_ref runs support corrected heating in serial\nparameter scans and 2D grids. Global beta and vtp , and each population's tauS , muS , alphS , Qs , Ds and vvS , retain their existing input meanings.\nScalar axes may appear in either order; both axes are validated, while scan_input_2 alone selects grid eigenfunctions and heating. The existing\nordered theta/magnitude geometry convention is retained. Each point is constructed from the segment or grid's initial native state, then\nthe derived solver normalization is refreshed. Initial root selection uses the\nfirst scan point. Inner-axis parameters are reset before continuing the outer\naxis; later serial segments retain the preceding segment's final parameters.\nFrequency derivatives hold the current plasma and physical wavevector fixed,\nnot a neighboring point along the parameter trajectory. Parameter bounds must be finite, with positive beta, vtp , temperature ratios,\nmass ratios and anisotropies, nonnegative density ratios, and charge ratios\nthat neither reach nor cross zero. Logarithmic coordinates require positive\nbounds; signed charge and drift scans can use linear spacing. Scan counts must\nbe positive and their product must fit the integration index. Duplicate grid\ntargets and overlapping wavevector axes are rejected. No wavevector floor is\nintroduced. Changing density, charge or drift does not automatically compensate another\npopulation or change reference frame. Charge/current imbalance and reference\ndrift produce warnings, not enforced neutrality conditions. A zero-density\npopulation has unavailable relative density/bulk moments and kinetic heating;\nits parameters and availability statuses remain in the output. Field/growth\napplicability restrictions still apply independently of scan support. Corrected parameter heating remains restricted to all-kinetic rho_ref runs;\nexisting d_ref parameter scans retain their historical behavior. d_ref beta\nand reference tau scans synchronize each other; independent 2D variation is\nrejected. Temperature scans cannot reach or leave tauS =0. Normalization explains why a beta scan follows different physics in the two conventions. Option =5 is experimental radial interpolation. It reads modelName0.rad for\nradius/beta/vtp and modelNameN.rad for each species' six parameters, and selects\none of seven k_range layouts using k_scan =0..6. \nIt is all-kinetic, with legacy\nradial heating only; corrected heating and cold species are not yet supported.\nInput fields and exact old record layouts are retained\nin Input and Output .","tags":"","url":"page/modes.html"},{"title":"Electromagnetic eigenfunctions and species moments – PLUME","text":"Electromagnetic eigenfunctions and species moments Manual home · Normalization · Output columns Field eigenfunction For the species susceptibilities ,\nPLUME forms and\nthe complete electromagnetic matrix , with . The definitions of , and are\non Normalization .\nA mode has and .\nThis uses all cold and kinetic populations;\nspecies-resolved tensors are retained. The historical solver constructs component ratios using two-by-two minors\n(cofactor elimination), with . It is not a general eigenvalue solver.\nThe cold-containing field module uses the same algebra, row scaling and equivalent\ncofactor pivots to handle cases such as exact-parallel transverse polarization.\nIt tests the null residual and rejects unresolved rank/gauge cases. The legacy\nall-kinetic path retains its arithmetic for exact comparisons. fixes the otherwise arbitrary overall complex amplitude and phase.\nThe relative complex component phases describe polarization.\nCompare component ratios or vectors aligned by an overall phase; unaligned raw vectors from another eigensolver need not match.\nCoordinate order is with along\npositive and .\nInterpret handedness using these axes and the convention. Magnetic conversion and units In Gaussian units physical Faraday's law is .\nFor , the output relationship is The last form applies only to a finite thermal reference with rho_ref inputs; and .\nThe first form applies to d_ref , including cold references. here is not normalized to .\nIf observations use SI, convert physical fields consistently before comparing electric/magnetic amplitudes. For and , , ,\nand .\nAt complex these are complex field ratios.\nHeating energy uses a distinct real-frequency magnetic reconstruction, discussed in Heating . is a restriction A mode with cannot be represented by this gauge. Examples include an\nexactly perpendicular cold ordinary mode and certain longitudinal/degenerate\npolarizations. A dispersion root can be valid while its field is unavailable.\nPLUME does not introduce another field normalization or a small floor.\nOff-mode matrices, unresolved nullvectors and nonfinite evaluations also yield\nunavailable fields. Do not mistake NaN for a physical zero polarization. This is another planned update to the code. For cold-containing fields, status meanings are: 0 success; 1 invalid\ninput/dispersion; 2 unresolved rank; 3 unavailable gauge; 4 no acceptable\nnullvector; 5 nonfinite arithmetic. The field module accepts a maximum\nrow-normalized null residual of and uses a gauge/rank check.\nThese checks are not guarantees of root accuracy or independent branch identity.\nA legacy kinetic output should still be checked for finite components and an\nindependent matrix residual where accuracy matters. Species density and velocity normalization Let , , and . These are exactly the complex velocity and\ndensity output slots. They are not velocity divided by thermal speed or ,\nand density is relative to that species' , not . The same normalization\napplies to rho_ref and d_ref , warm and cold references, and either model. Kinetic current/continuity method The existing kinetic moment calculation obtains the normalized current from\nthat population's ordinary susceptibility at the accepted complex mode : Here is the signed input charge ratio, the density ratio and the\nAlfvén-normalized parallel drift. includes the equilibrium-density current.\nThis compact current removal/continuity form is used in the mixed path and is\nalgebraically equivalent to the historical kinetic formulas where their\nintermediate divisions are regular. It reuses each kinetic susceptibility,\nnot a duplicate harmonic moment solver. The all-kinetic path preserves its\nhistorical arithmetic. For relative density/bulk moments of an absent\npopulation are undefined and marked unavailable on guarded paths. Cold-fluid momentum and continuity Define and .\nThe independently normalized cold equations are The magnetic drift force is essential for oblique waves. PLUME solves the\nthree-component momentum matrix and then continuity, without thermal speeds, or Bessel sums. No cold anisotropy placeholder enters. The signs follow the\nsame Fourier convention and signed charge as the susceptibility kernel.\nExact and are poles; no frequency offset or damping hides them.\nNearby finite complex frequencies are evaluated directly. The complete species current is Dropping the density-drift term breaks consistency even if the transverse\nvelocities look plausible. The cold moment kernel is independently validated\nagainst this susceptibility current, momentum and continuity. Individual\nspecies indices remain those of the input; neither electrons nor cold species\nare assumed to occupy particular slots. Requests, failure isolation and examples Serial scans use eigen=.true. in the relevant scan_input_N ; two-dimensional\ngrids use scan_input_2 for eigen/heating flags. Direct option=-1 attempts\navailable diagnostics at the supplied frequency. option=1 with nscan=0 does\nnot write a standalone eigenfunction table. Map eigen/tensor requests are\nignored with warnings, not converted into diagnostic files. Cold-containing dispatch calculates fields, then each species' warm or cold\nmoment. A failed species moment leaves that block NaN without overwriting valid or other species. A failed field leaves all associated moments unavailable. Moment statuses: 0 valid; 1 input/classification; 2 zero ; 3 cold Doppler pole; 4 cold cyclotron pole; 5 nonfinite arithmetic; 6 absent population. Diagnostics must not contaminate subsequent scan points. \nCorrected heating has separately isolated state and cannot invalidate accepted roots/moments on failure. mkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_fields_cold_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/cold_fields_parallel.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/mixed_moments_multispecies.in These bounded examples write plus kinetic/fluid moments in existing mode\ncolumns; see Examples for their actual inputs and benchmarks.\nThe cold parallel field example's first written root is approximately in units of , with imaginary part about (numerical\nroundoff about a real cold branch). It is a transverse circular mode; no\nkinetic damping should be inferred from that tiny imaginary value. For an independent check, reconstruct from species and compare , or the stricter row-scaled residual used by\nthe field module. Verify Faraday's law and align any independent nullvector to . A small raw determinant alone is insufficient when the matrix is badly\nscaled. Output gives the column order; pressure perturbations,\nnew gauge choices and cold kinetic heating/FPC are not implemented.","tags":"","url":"page/eigenfunctions.html"},{"title":"Kinetic absorption/emission and electromagnetic wave energy – PLUME","text":"Kinetic absorption/emission and electromagnetic wave energy Manual home · Eigenfunctions · Energy file schema What is calculated heating_convention='corrected' is the default, independently of method,\nBessel mode, and input normalization. \nIt supports collisionless kinetic, cold, and mixed plasmas, \nincluding cold references, at single-wavevector diagnostic calls and serial/2D wavevector scans. \nCollisionless, all-kinetic rho_ref runs also support serial and 2D plasma-parameter\nscans, with the current plasma held fixed for each frequency derivative. See scan semantics .\nEach warm population retains its full species absorption/emission diagnostic and the original seven selected channels. \nEvery physical species, including cold ones, contributes to formal wave energy. These are weak-mode linear response diagnostics, not dimensional thermodynamic\nheating power, entropy production or an exact conserved energy for arbitrary\nunstable waves. Cold-fluid reversible energy exchange is not kinetic heating.\nCold species' and seven channels are NaN; a fully cold run has no available\nkinetic-heating total. This differs physically from a calculated zero for a\nwarm population at a nonabsorbing point. The accepted mode and real-frequency snapshots Use the normalized mode , , , and , , from the full-plasma accepted complex-frequency eigenfunction. In this section means ; in formulas involving is likewise normalized. The susceptibility\nsnapshots below are evaluated at real laboratory , with the same fixed\nphysical wavevector and plasma. We do not replace that laboratory frequency by\na species Doppler frequency for the energy derivative. Define and . The dagger is complex conjugate\ntranspose; this is distinct from the ordinary tensor transpose symmetries.\nThe unnormalized absorption/emission quadratic form is The companion column Q_sN denotes this numerator for species , not the\nsigned input charge ratio Qs . It is evaluated locally without overwriting the\naccepted-mode global susceptibility or wave matrix. Complete formal wave energy For the conventional real-frequency homogeneous-plasma weak-mode expression, is in Gaussian physical field-energy units for the chosen physical . and its terms are dimensionless. is the vacuum electric-field term\nfrom differentiating the identity in . uses real even when the\nmode has small nonzero ; it is not obtained by blindly reusing the magnetic\nfield evaluated with complex . includes the full species dielectric\nresponse, drifts and anisotropy. Every cold/kinetic species contributes once.\nNo auxiliary normalization scale is a population or energy term. The derivative fixes , species densities/temperatures/anisotropies/drifts,\nbackground and reference scales. Dimensionless differentiation introduces no\nextra factor because . The expression\nis conventional for weakly damped/growing isolated modes; its continuation as a\nformal quadratic form is not an exact strong-mode conservation law. For stationary collisionless cold species at real frequency away from poles,\nthe same expression has an independent bulk-energy identity: Here is the normalized fluid velocity defined on Eigenfunctions .\nThe second expression is time-averaged oscillatory bulk kinetic energy for\ncomplex-amplitude perturbations; multiplying by gives it. This is reversible cold bulk energy, not kinetic heating. The simple\npositive identity assumes zero equilibrium drift; drifting formal energy need\nnot be positive and must use the full dielectric derivative. Signed fractions and the electric magnitude measure The companion exposes H_total , H_E , H_B , H_species_total and each , together\nwith , , and . These are signed\nfractions of the formal quadratic form . Negative-energy/drifting modes may\nhave negative terms or fractions outside . They are not clamped. When is resolved, within numerical precision. A separate noncancelling contribution measure is It measures electric contribution relative to individual term magnitudes; it\nis not a physical energy fraction. It is useful when signed terms nearly\ncancel. A zero/nonfinite denominator is unavailable. Negative is not itself\nfailure; unresolved or near-zero relative to its numerical uncertainty\nmakes signed fractions and normalized unavailable, without a small floor. Species normalization and energy consistency The corrected dimensionless species diagnostic is There is no extra , wave period, or factor of two. For a weak isolated\nmode, mutually consistent field/particle energy transfer gives approximately Cold collisionless real-frequency response is Hermitian away from poles; cold\nspecies supply reversible energy in , not a kinetic absorption channel in that\nsum. This approximate relation is independently checked , not imposed to\ncreate closure. Reactive/degenerate instabilities, strong damping, branch\ninterference or unresolved responses can invalidate the interpretation.\nA good energy-balance residual alone does not prove applicability. The seven selected channels Each numerator uses the same snapshots and the accepted mode's , then\nis divided by the same as the full species . Here denotes the\nsingle signed harmonic susceptibility at real , , and\nconjugate field factors are explicit. Channel Unnormalized numerator Historical interpretation 1 TTD 2 TTD cross 3 LD cross 4 LD 5 Full 6 Transverse cyclotron 7 Transverse cyclotron TTD denotes transit-time damping and LD Landau damping in this diagnostic\nnaming; the signed quantities can also describe emission. At the corresponding tensor structure, channels 1–4 sum to channel 5. Channels 5–7 do not generally sum to species-total : higher harmonics and longitudinal/cross\ncomponents are omitted intentionally. Summing all seven double-counts .\nThe full species total remains separate. These selected diagnostics are not\nredefined to force total closure. Corrected snapshots initialize every harmonic tensor entry, including the five\nunused entries, and retain exact-parallel analytic limits where the\nmixed/cold dispersion supports them. There are no / floors. Derivative\ncalls cannot overwrite the central total/harmonic snapshots. low_n controls\nwhether seven channels are written in the old mode block; the corrected companion\nstill contains all seven. FPC harmonic evaluation is a different calculation. Derivatives, convergence and isolation Kinetic energy derivatives use centered differences of and Richardson\nextrapolation. For a species let , fixed /plasma, and : The implementation tries 13 halving relative steps from to ,\nrequiring successive estimates to resolve both matrix Frobenius norm and the -contracted scalar at tolerance , with comparison scales .\nIt selects the best stable window, not necessarily the smallest step. Exported dmatrix_s , dproject_s and dstep_s describe that window; uncertainty is an\nempirical numerical resolution estimate, not a rigorous error bound. Poles,\nBessel/ errors and cancellation can defeat convergence. No fallback silently\nsubstitutes the historical normalizer. Cold derivatives use the analytic differentiation of the normalized fluid\nmomentum/continuity response. Exact real-frequency poles remain failures.\nThe finite-difference sampling of kinetic species does not unnecessarily evaluate\ncold responses at off-center frequencies. Corrected diagnostic work is local:\naccepted-mode susc , susc_low , lam and dispersion status remain at the converged\nmode. Failed energy/heating does not invalidate root, field or species moments. Availability, applicability and statuses Corrected diagnostics require collision_type=0 . They reject finite-rate physics\nrather than making new collisional claims. Legacy all-kinetic collisional\nbehavior can be requested explicitly, with known limitations . Failures include , real cold poles, nonfinite response, unresolved derivative,\nunresolved and unavailable field. A valid complex dispersion root may\ntherefore have unavailable real-frequency energy/heating. A cold singularity at is not concealed by the mode's finite imaginary part. Applicability bit 1 is set for . Bit 2 flags weak-balance or\nnear-reactive inconsistency. Formal energy can remain resolved ( energy_status=0 )\nwhile an applicability bit makes normalized warm /channels NaN. Raw and terms remain informative but must not be presented as validated thermodynamic\nheating. A reactive mode is not repaired by dividing by an artificial floor.\nNumerical energy status and physical applicability are separate fields; the output status tables list both. Running and reading existing examples mkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_kinetic.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_warm_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_cold_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_cold.in Existing input First printed mode frequency Formal near first point heating_corrected_kinetic heating_corrected_warm_reference heating_corrected_cold_reference heating_corrected_cold plus roundoff imaginary part These bounded inputs and numbers are verified against current output and the\nindependent heating tests; they are not universal branch frequencies. Inspect\nstatuses as well as values. For a separate moderate- test with artificial mass\nratio , the independent suite finds about . That is a test\nbenchmark, not the physical mass ratio in the supplied cold example; the\ncurated public moderate- input is still outstanding. from linfpclib.linfpc import load_plume_energy r = load_plume_energy ( 'data/example/heating_corrected_cold_reference.energy' ) print ( r [ 'H_total' ], r [ 'H_E' ], r [ 'f_E' ], r [ 'f_E_abs' ]) print ( r [ 'energy_status' ], r [ 'applicability' ]) print ( r [ 'H_s1' ], r [ 'Ps_s1' ]) # cold proton energy exists; kinetic Ps is NaN New energy columns are in the versioned .energy companion, not inserted in\nthe old low_n=true heating blocks or in the old unassigned Ew slot. Output\nassociation and all status fields are specified in Output . Legacy convention heating_convention='legacy' preserves historical all-kinetic calc_eigen arithmetic, old normalizer (without vacuum electric energy), one-sided derivative,\nshifted harmonic snapshots and legacy scratch/output behavior. It does not\nsilently inherit corrected mathematics, and cold/mixed legacy heating is not\navailable. It is for reproducibility, not a second accurate complete energy\nconvention. See Historical reproduction for all three\nindependent switches and the separate role of a source revision. Scientific context: PLUME (Klein, Howes & Brown, 2025) and Huang et al. (2024) .","tags":"","url":"page/heating.html"},{"title":"Output files and Python reading – PLUME","text":"Output files and Python reading Manual home · Modes · Normalization Choose the file by workflow Let D= dataName , O= outputName , S= nspec , and N the branch slot (one-based).\nAll paths below are relative to the execution directory. No single reader\nhandles every family merely because a filename contains .mode or .eigen . Producer Filename pattern Contents / primary reader Direct option=-1 stdout Labeled determinant/fields/moments/diagnostics; not a rectangular mode table Frequency map data/D/dispersion_O.map Five-column determinant grid; NumPy loadtxt Map candidates data/D/dispersion_O.roots Coordinates/root plus species parameters; NumPy loadtxt Repeated map dispersion_O_<scan-tag>.map/.roots Same schemas at each scanned state Serial scan data/D/O_<parameter>_<range>.modeN Mode columns selected by flags; explicit sweep/contour reader 2D scan data/D/O_<parameter1>_<parameter2>.modeN Same mode schema, blank-separated outer blocks Scan tensors O_tensor_<parameter>_<range>.modeN or 2D counterpart Six independent complex chi components per species Corrected energy/heating data/D/O.energy Versioned named-column companion, load_plume_energy d_ref metadata data/D/normalization_O.txt Native units/composition/auxiliary and moment notes Bessel metadata data/D/O.bessel Harmonic policy/cache/convergence metadata FPC options 6/7 O.<quantity>.specieSS.modeMM Thermal/grid headers and velocity blocks, specialized readers FPC debug O.eigen.modeMM , O.mom.modeMM Historical debug layouts; not scan mode schemas Radial option=5 data/D/O.modeN and associated historical outputs Radius-last experimental layouts; explicit custom parser The integer scan tags scale/truncate coordinate values; they are not units and\ncan collide for nearby endpoints. Treat numeric coordinate columns and matching\ninput as authoritative. Use unique O/run directories and completed-file checks\nto avoid stale branches. writeOut=.false. does not prevent files or metadata.\nSupplied-root option=1/nscan=0 prints solutions but does not write a standalone\n.roots file. Map files do not gain eigen/heating/tensor blocks when flags request\nthem; ignored map diagnostics now warn, and corrected map heating is rejected. Warning output Warnings are written to data/D/O.warn , including equilibrium, scan,\nfield/moment, heating/energy, Bessel and FPC warnings. The file is replaced after\nreading &params on each run and is empty if no warnings occur. Records are\nflushed as they are written, including before an abnormal termination. Set warnings_to_stdout=.true. in &params to also print warnings to stdout.\nThe default is .false. , independently of writeOut . The Python wrapper accepts warnings_to_stdout=True in set_params and preserves the namelist control on\nread/write roundtrips. Fatal errors and ordinary\nprogress and result output retain their existing destinations. Warning conditions,\nstatus codes and numerical results are unchanged. The output directory must already exist. If the warning log cannot be opened or\nwritten, an I/O warning is reported once on stderr and subsequent warnings are\nsent there. Warnings emitted before log initialization also use stderr. Maps and candidate roots .map No header. Each real-frequency block has ni+1 rows, followed by a blank line;\nthere are nr+1 blocks. Exactly five columns: One-based column Meaning 1 omega_r/Omega_R 2 gamma/Omega_R 3 log10(abs(det Lambda)) 4 Re det Lambda 5 Im det Lambda Lambda is the ordinary electromagnetic wave matrix, not chi or a frequency-scaled\ntensor. Invalid guarded map samples are NaN and not legitimate minima.\nAn exactly zero finite determinant has logarithmic magnitude -infinity;\ndistinguish it from a NaN failed evaluation. Real/imaginary map coordinate\nprecision is es14.6 and determinant components es14.4. The log-grid inconsistency means\nlogarithmic bounds are not physical endpoints; linear grids are recommended. .roots No header; width 6+6S, typically es14.4. The first six columns are\nkperp, kpar, betap, vtp, Re w, Im w. Then each population contributes\n(tauS, muS, alphS, Qs, Ds, vvS) in original order. k/beta/vtp/tau use the selected\nnative input convention; w always uses Omega_R. These are refined/deduplicated map candidates before later selection filters,\nnot a promise of the first nroot_max least-damped modes. Later filtering and\nscan continuation do not rewrite .roots into an accepted-branch table. Failed\nrefinements may be omitted. Compare with root-search semantics . Mode files The serial/2D .modeN writer has no header and uses es15.6e3 numeric formatting.\nLet Eflag be eigen, Pflag be heating, Lflag be low_n. The first six columns always\ncontain native kperp, kpar, betap, vtp, Re w, Im w. Each complex quantity is\nrepresented by real then imaginary parts. Species are always in input order. For unambiguous indexing below use zero-based population s=0..S-1 and component\nj=0,1,2 (x,y,z). Offsets below are zero-based Python indices, not printed column\nnumbers. Block Start Length / ordering Coordinate/root prefix 0 6 B, if Eflag 6 Bx_re,Bx_im,By_re,By_im,Bz_re,Bz_im E, if Eflag 12 Ex_re,Ex_im,Ey_re,Ey_im,Ez_re,Ez_im Species velocity, if Eflag 18 6S: all xyz real/imag pairs for species 1, then 2, etc. Species density, if Eflag 18+6S 2S: density real/imag pair per species Species heating totals, if Pflag F S: Ps_1..Ps_S Selected channels, if Pflag and Lflag F+S 7S: seven channels for species 1, then 2, etc. Species input parameters F+K 6S: tau,mu,alpha,Q,D,drift per species Historical Ew slot F+K+6S One slot only if Eflag and Pflag and not Lflag Here F=18+8S when Eflag, otherwise 6. K=8S when Pflag and Lflag, S when\nPflag and not Lflag, and zero when not Pflag. Velocity component real/imag\nindices are 18+6s+2j and the next index; density indices are 18+6S+2s and next.\nAll field/moment units are defined in Eigenfunctions . Flags Rectangular column count No diagnostics 6+6S Eigen only 18+14S Heating only, low_n=true 6+14S Eigen + heating, low_n=true 18+22S Heating only, low_n=false (corrected) 6+7S Eigen + heating, low_n=false 19+15S When low_n=true, total Ps_s is at F+s and channel c=0..6 at F+S+7s+c. The\nchannels are n0 yy, n0 yz, n0 zy, n0 zz, full n0, transverse +1, transverse -1.\nThe physical definitions are on Heating .\nThe parameter suffix always follows the full selected heating block, including\nheating-only files. It must not be mistaken for extra heating columns. Historical Ew was not assigned by calc_eigen and is not wave energy .\nCorrected eigen+heating/low_n=false explicitly writes NaN in that old slot.\nLegacy heating-only/low_n=false can emit an extra orphan unassigned Ew record,\nmaking the file ragged. No generic rectangular parser should silently treat\nthat as a new root. Do not use it as corrected H; read .energy . Two-dimensional output has blank outer-block separators. np.loadtxt and the\nordinary sweep reader flatten these into rows, so retain coordinates for grid\nreconstruction rather than assuming missing/failed points can be removed.\n2D eigen/heating switches come from scan_input_2; tensor output requires both\ntensor switches. Serial files use the corresponding scan's flags. Failed written roots have NaN frequency and diagnostic slots with input parameters\nretained. Cold kinetic-heating slots are also NaN, but for a different reason.\nA field failure can coexist with a valid root; a species moment failure does not\noverwrite valid fields or other species. Use companion statuses and warnings,\nnot a blanket replacement of missing values with zero. Illustrated mode columns The diagrams below map quantities to one-based column numbers within a\nsingle output row for two, three and four species, with eigen=.true. , heating=.true. and low_n=.true. . Each complex quantity occupies a real/imaginary\npair. Species are numbered in input order; denotes reference species 1.\nVelocity components are ordered for every species. The coordinate and species-parameter labels use rho_ref ; d_ref retains the\ncolumn ordering with the native units defined in Normalization .\nField and moment normalizations are defined in Eigenfunctions .\nThe heating labels follow heating_convention='corrected' , with separate\ntransverse and channels; see Heating .\nLegacy heating retains the column ordering but uses its historical definitions.\nThe corrected .energy companion has its own schema below. Two species: 62 columns. Open full-size diagram . Three species: 84 columns. Open full-size diagram . Four species: 106 columns. Open full-size diagram . Regenerating the diagrams LaTeX sources: two species , three species , and four species .\nWith LaTeX (including standalone ) and pdf2svg installed, run from the repository\nroot to regenerate all three SVGs; intermediate files remain in a temporary directory: table_build = $( mktemp -d ) for suffix in '' '-2' '-3' ; do pdflatex -interaction = nonstopmode -halt-on-error \\ -output-directory = \" $table_build \" \"docs/assets/PLUME-Table ${ suffix } .tex\" || break pdf2svg \" $table_build /PLUME-Table ${ suffix } .pdf\" \\ \"docs/assets/PLUME-Output-Table ${ suffix } .svg\" || break done Susceptibility files The tensor writer produces 4+18S columns , no frequency columns and no header: kperp, kpar, betap, vtp (native inputs). Complex chi_xx for all species, then chi_xy for all, chi_xz, chi_yy, chi_yz,\n   chi_zz, in that component-major order. Six native input parameters per species, species-major as above. For component c=0..5 and species s=0..S-1, its real/imag pair starts at\n4+2(cS+s). Parameter suffix begins at 4+12S. The other elements satisfy\nchi_yx=-chi_xy, chi_zx=chi_xz, chi_zy=-chi_yz, with ordinary transpose/sign, not complex conjugation . These are dimensionless species chi contributions\nwithout a vacuum identity. To reconstruct epsilon and Lambda, add each once and\nuse the frequency in the matching mode row. A tensor file alone does not supply\nthat mode frequency. Failed guarded evaluations yield NaNs, not a cached tensor\nfrom a prior successful point. Repeated maps do not emit these files. Energy companion Corrected direct option=-1 and serial/2D scans requesting heating write data/D/O.energy . The file has a # schema=plume-energy-v1 header, numerical\nsettings/species model notes, and a # columns: line. It is a whitespace table\nwith 27+15S numeric fields and one final quoted string . Do not split the\nfinal filename on spaces without respecting quoting. Exact header order: record workflow scan outer point mode root_valid field_status\nenergy_status applicability response_code\nkperp kpar omega_r gamma H_total H_E H_B H_species_total\nf_E f_B f_species f_E_abs uncertainty gamma_ratio gamma_est total_Ps For each species N=1..S the next 15 fields are: H_sN f_sN Q_sN Ps_sN status_sN dmatrix_sN dproject_sN dstep_sN\nC1_sN C2_sN C3_sN C4_sN C5_sN C6_sN C7_sN The final field is source_file . The N above is a species number, e.g. H_s1,\nnot the literal character N in a header. Eleven leading fields are integers;\nspecies status is written numerically among the real-valued species block but\nis a categorical code. Use the actual # columns header for reading. Field(s) Meaning record Monotone record counter within this companion workflow -1 direct, 1 serial, 2 grid scan, outer, point, mode Original scan/outer/internal integration/branch indices; zero when not used root_valid Caller validity indicator; in direct -1, field acceptance at supplied frequency is used, not an independent root proof field_status Ex=1 field diagnostic status energy_status Numerical resolution of energy, table below applicability Bit mask for weak-mode interpretation response_code Underlying response/pole/numerical status when relevant kperp,kpar,omega_r,gamma Native k and Omega_R-normalized frequency H_total,H_E,H_B,H_species_total,H_sN Dimensionless complete formal energy and contributions f_E,f_B,f_species,f_sN Signed formal fractions, not clamped f_E_abs Noncancelling electric contribution measure, not a physical fraction uncertainty Empirical numerical H resolution estimate, not a rigorous bound gamma_ratio abs(gamma/omega_r) gamma_est Independent weak-mode estimate -omega_r sum(Q_warm)/H total_Ps Sum of available warm normalized Ps; fully cold/inapplicable unavailable, not fabricated zero Q_sN,Ps_sN,C1_sN..C7_sN Raw absorption numerator, normalized full and seven selected species channels status_sN Kinetic/cold/absent/numerical/applicability availability category dmatrix_sN,dproject_sN,dstep_sN Derivative window error indicators and chosen relative step; cold analytic derivatives use zero source_file Quoted associated mode filename, or stdout for direct evaluation H terms are in units 16 pi W/abs(deltaEx)&#94;2; definitions and weak-mode limitations\nare on Heating . Cold H_s may be valid while its Q/Ps/channels are\nunavailable. Central snapshots use real frequency; accepted-mode fields/moments\nremain complex-frequency diagnostics. The companion does not redefine Ew. energy_status Meaning 0 Numerically resolved formal energy 1 Invalid input 2 Unavailable/invalid Ex=1 field 3 Zero real frequency 4 Response failure or real pole 5 Unresolved derivative 6 Unresolved/vanishing H 7 Unsupported collision configuration 8 Failed root 9 Nonfinite arithmetic Applicability bits: 1 means abs(gamma/omega_r)>.05; 2 means weak-balance or\nnear-reactive warning. Both may be set. Formal H can be resolved while normalized\nPs/channels are unavailable. Negative H alone is not failure. Species statuses:\n0 valid kinetic, 1 cold kinetic heating unavailable, 2 absent, 3 input/response\nfailure, 4 derivative, 5 unavailable energy normalizer, 6 applicability. For corrected rho_ref parameter scans, comment records preserve the changing\nplasma without changing plume-energy-v1 numeric columns: # scan_axis records the scan index, style/type, bounds, counts and spacing at\n  the start of a workflow or serial segment. # point_state record=N betap=... vtp=... gives the native beta and velocity\n  control for energy record N . # species_state record=N species=S tauS=... muS=... alphS=... Qs=... Ds=... vvS=... gives all six native inputs for species S at that record. Values use the companion's full numeric precision. Wavevector and frequency are\nalready in the associated energy row. These comments are written for failed\npoints as well as successful ones; existing readers continue to ignore them.\nThe .modeN parameter suffix retains its established ordering and precision. Joining energy and modes point is an internal integration index, not the output row number when\nnres>1. Use source_file, mode, scan/outer/point and numeric coordinates to join.\nCompleted scans write companion entries for failed written roots (status 8),\nso failures do not shift subsequent associations. Hidden failed substeps warn\nbut have no written record. Interrupted processes can flush a companion before\na buffered mode file, so verify completion and row counts before joining.\nCompanions are reset for direct/serial/grid starts to avoid stale records;\nlegacy runs do not write a replacement corrected companion. Never merge old\nbranches by an unrestricted folder glob and presume they belong to this run. Metadata normalization_O.txt Written for d_ref as data/D/normalization_O.txt . It identifies native units,\nreference/composition, species moment conventions, availability and, for a cold\nreference with warm populations, the internal first-warm auxiliary scale.\nReusing an output name with rho_ref removes stale d_ref metadata. Absence alone\nis not proof that arbitrary old/external data are rho_ref; keep the original input.\nSome historical prose in metadata is broader than the current supported paths;\nuse this manual and explicit flags for diagnostic availability. O.bessel Normally written at executable completion. Key/value lines identify harmonic mode,\ntolerance, adaptive maximum/fixed count and unresolved convergence. With\nbessel_diagnostics=true or any unresolved adaptive request, cached-species rows are species lambda N capacity converged E0_bound E2_bound hits misses array_evaluations Optional unresolved history has species, maximum, lambda, tolerance, E0_bound,\nE2_bound. Active N and allocation capacity differ. Counters count an array\nevaluation, not each coefficient, and a cache miss can require selection without\nnew arrays. Cold/cache-unused slots may be absent. The historical fixed128 path\nexplicitly does not pretend to have measured modern cache counters.\nZ backend is printed with writeOut and recorded in corrected energy metadata;\nkeep the input for complete provenance of runs without energy output. FPC and debug families Gyro suffixes: .cpar , .cperp , .df1gyro.real , .df1gyro.imag , followed by .specieSS.modeMM . Headers contain eight labels/values (tau, bi, kpar, kperp,\nvti, mu, omega.r, omega.i), five grid labels/values and a separator. The velocity\nmatrix has vperp rows and vpar columns. Bounds are inclusive according to the\nwriter's integer-grid construction. Header wave quantities use the derived\nthermal solver convention , even from d_ref input. The omega fields literally\nstore w sqrt(beta_R)/Kpar rather than a dimensionless frequency alone; this is\nnot the physical phase speed in v_A units when alpha_R differs from one. Cartesian suffixes include .cparcart , .cperp1 , .cperp2 , .dfs.real , .dfs.imag , .dfs3D.real , .dfs3D.imag , with analogous indexed suffixes.\nThe projection reader handles three matrices (vx,vy), (vx,vz), (vy,vz), with\nseven grid bounds/spacing values. Three-dimensional blocks have their own\nstorage order (z,y,x), one writer line per (x,y) containing z samples. They are\nnot interchangeable with projected files. Consult the FPC appendix for the existing correlation/distribution units\nand orientation details; do not infer them from a generic scan reader. Current gyro/Cartesian readers preserve NaNs, infinities, finite signed values,\nphysical zeros and original shapes. They offer the explicitly optional visualization_nan_to_zero=True for plots only. It replaces only NaNs ;\nthe 3D default also preserves representable tails rather than clipping them.\nHistorical species-label/header-offset heuristics remain. These functions do\nnot add mixed FPC or validate all FPC moment/energy closure questions. Debug .eigen.modeMM has 18+16S values: six root/plasma entries, 12 fields,\n6S velocities, 2S densities, S old Ps and 7S selected channels, without species\nparameter suffix . Debug .mom.modeMM has 11S values: density complex for all,\nvelocities complex for all, then S values each of jxEx,jyEy,jzEz in the historical\ndebug definition. loadeigen / loadmoms have hard-coded/debug assumptions,\nincluding fabricated electron-P behavior in loadeigen. Do not recommend them\nfor general species interpretation or energy closure. Experimental radial output Option=5 writes a distinct radius-last format, not the ordinary scan schema.\nAfter the native six coordinate/root entries, it may write fields/moments and\nspecies-total-only legacy heat, then 6S parameters, old unassigned Ew if heating,\nand finally radius in solar radii. Root-only width is 7+6S; eigen-only 19+14S;\nheating-only 8+7S; eigen+heating 20+15S. Do not silently discard the last radius\nor assume seven channels are present. These experimental layouts need a custom\nparser; there is no validated primary standalone radial example. Python workflows Run Python from the repository root or add that root to the import path. The\nwrapper remains optional. Use explicit schema settings rather than counting\nsurplus columns as diagnostics. from linfpclib.linfpc import load_plume_sweep , load_plume_energy # The Quick Start eigen-only file: species parameters are NOT heating. d = load_plume_sweep ( 'data/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference_kpar_60_90.mode1' , nspec = 2 , eigen = True , heating = False ) w = d [ 'w' ] + 1 j * d [ 'g' ] # For a corrected heating run use heating=True and its matching eigen flag. r = load_plume_energy ( 'data/example/heating_corrected_warm_reference.energy' ) print ( r [ 'energy_status' ], r [ 'H_total' ], r [ 'f_E' ]) The generic current sweep reader correctly uses seven-channel stride and\nheating-only offsets for low_n=true . It is not a general low_n=false, radial,\nmap or FPC-debug reader. load_plume_energy uses the versioned header, preserves\nfailed rows/NaNs and quoted source paths, and returns named NumPy columns plus\nmetadata. Cold kinetic-heating NaN is preserved by both appropriate readers. The contour/resonance reader offers stricter explicit schemas: from pathlib import Path from contours.plot_plume_resonances import read_plume_input , read_dispersion_output meta = read_plume_input ( Path ( 'inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in' )) disp = read_dispersion_output ( Path ( 'data/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference_kpar_60_90.mode1' ), meta , 1 ) assert not disp . heating_available For serial inputs with unequal scan diagnostic flags, pass one-based scan_index=N to that API. The plotter CLI does not yet expose the selector;\nambiguous layouts are rejected. 2D uses the second scan flags. Wrong widths,\nwrong file families and ragged legacy orphan records raise useful errors.\nAll seven heating channels and original species order are retained when the\nexplicit input requests them. Rectangle low_n=false files are interpreted with\nthe matching flags; no heat is inferred just because more columns remain. FPC reading uses the specialized family, for example: from linfpclib.linfpc import loadlinfpccepar , loadlinfpccart # Use actual option6/7 files, not .modeN scan files: # gyro = loadlinfpccepar('data/example/<actual>.cpar.specie01.mode01') # cart = loadlinfpccart('data/example/<actual>.cparcart.specie01.mode01') # visualization_nan_to_zero=True is an explicit plotting-only opt-in. The filenames are placeholders here because this manual does not create a new\npublic FPC example. The existing notebook and appendix provide the historical\nworkflow; FPC is not enabled for a warm member of a mixed plasma. For scalar input roundtrips: from linfpclib.linfpc import plume_input obj = plume_input ( 'constructor_fallback' ) obj . load_from_file ( 'inputs/example/mixed_fields_warm_reference.in' ) obj . dataname = 'example' # explicit post-load redirection; loaded dataName is preserved obj . write_input ( '/tmp/my_plume_case' , 'my_plume_case' ) # creates /tmp/my_plume_case.in The scalar supported groups include low_n, loggridg, nr/ni, tensor and Cartesian\nbounds. Unknown controls, radial/k_range groups and array/expression syntax are\nrejected instead of silently lost. Repeated load replaces prior species/scans.\nwriteOut and use_map remain independent verbosity/initialization settings; they\nare not a combined “write files” flag. See Input for accepted scalar syntax and setter options. Historical plotters in plotter/ , SWIFT and debug helpers can assume fixed\nspecies counts, rho labels or old column contracts. Inspect those assumptions\nbefore using them on new compositions. A plausible plot is not schema validation.","tags":"","url":"page/output.html"},{"title":"Examples: a twelve-lesson curriculum – PLUME","text":"Examples: a twelve-lesson curriculum Manual home · Quick Start · Modes All commands below assume the repository root, a completed make -j2 , and mkdir -p data/example . Links point to existing input sources; no new input\nfiles are invented by this manual. Match each reader to the file's species count\nand flags. Benchmarks below are current locally verified printed values or\nexplicitly labeled developer references, not universal dispersion relations.\nA process finishing is not by itself validation of every mode/diagnostic. Lessons with existing inputs Lesson Input and physical purpose Normalization / populations Run and inspect 1. Standard kinetic plasma and equivalent units normalization_rho_ref.in , normalization_d_ref.in Equivalent rho_ref/d_ref proton-electron inputs; alpha_R=1.5, electron drift .02 Run both files; compare converted k columns, all three frequencies and E/B/moments; first w=.06024424-.0004186511i 2. Low-frequency Alfvénic/kinetic-Alfvén response and channels heating_corrected_kinetic.in rho_ref, two warm populations, anisotropic reference/electrons Run file; inspect .mode1 and .energy; first w=.06098715-1.270997e-5i, H about 20342.68; identify branch by polarization, not filename alone 3. Cold parallel ion-cyclotron/circular response cold_fields_parallel.in , cold_proton_electron.in d_ref, stationary cold proton/electron, finite electron mass Field scan: first w about .05820986 with roundoff imaginary part; compare cold circular n&#94;2=R/L and transverse polarization. The single supplied-root file only prints roots 4. Fully cold perpendicular electromagnetic mode cold_proton_electron_perpendicular.in d_ref, fully cold, exact kpar=0, single wavevector Run file; inspect printed root against O/X relations. Do not infer perpendicular scans or an Ex=1 O-mode eigenfunction are supported 5. Warm ions / cold electrons heating_corrected_warm_reference.in d_ref, warm reference, cold electrons .mode1 and .energy; first w=.06153842-5.564326e-6i, H about 20384.80; electron Ps/channels NaN while H_e is present 6. Cold ions / warm electrons heating_corrected_cold_reference.in d_ref, betap=0, cold protons, warm electrons First w=.05916735-1.588769e-6i, H about 20516.88; proton kinetic heat NaN, electron heat available; auxiliary scale is not physical temperature 7. Several populations and parallel drift currents mixed_moments_multispecies.in d_ref, interleaved cold protons, warm electrons/alphas, cold alphas First w=.05440212-1.366260e-6i; inspect every species' U/n; reconstruct current including density times equilibrium drift 8. Temperature-anisotropy instability example_anisotropy_par.in rho_ref , warm protons/electrons; , , isotropic electrons, no drifts Inspect .mode1 , .mode2 and .energy ; locate intervals with , identify the unstable branch by polarization, and check heating applicability before interpreting absorption 9. Whistler/R branch example_anisotropy_par.in Same plasma and scan as lesson 8; two supplied frequency guesses Follow the whistler/R branch using its frequency and field polarization as increases; check branch continuity and warnings rather than identifying the branch by mode slot alone 10. Two-dimensional scale and beta scan example_simple_grid.in rho_ref , warm protons/electrons; logarithmic and axes at fixed Inspect simple_grid_kperp_betap.mode1 , simple_grid.energy and simple_grid.warn ; reconstruct the 2D grid from coordinate columns and check energy status/applicability before interpreting heating 11. Vacuum electric energy and fully cold energy heating_corrected_cold.in d_ref, physical proton/electron mass ratio, stationary fully cold First w about .05972252; H about 20607.63; H_E about 1.00721 and f_E about 4.88755e-5; kinetic heat remains unavailable 12. Historical numerical/diagnostic replay heating_legacy.in rho_ref all kinetic; explicit legacy Z/fixed128/legacy heat Inspect old mode Ps/channels, not a corrected .energy; record source revision and all flags For example: ./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_warm_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_corrected_cold_reference.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/example_anisotropy_par.in\n./plume.e inputs/example/example_simple_grid.in Lessons 8 and 9 share a logarithmic scan from to , at fixed . The input requests 400 output\nsteps and 32 substeps per output step for each of two branches, with eigenfunctions\nand corrected heating enabled. This is a longer continuation example than the\nbounded single-branch inputs above; inspect failed points and diagnostic statuses\nthroughout the scan. Lesson 10 scans from to and from 1 to 10, with corrected heating and eigenfunctions.\nIts 200 by 30 output intervals produce a grid, or 6,231 rows\nper branch. The .energy companion records exact parameter states in comments.\nValid roots can have unavailable heating; inspect the companion statuses and\nwarning log throughout the grid. Energy is only a formal weak-mode diagnostic; check energy_status and applicability\nin the companion before interpreting normalized absorption. Lesson 11's ordinary\ncold example has a small electric fraction. The separate test-only moderate-a,\nartificial mass-ratio-4 case has f_E about .500000281; do not attribute that number\nto the supplied physical-mass-ratio example. Creating its explicit public input\nis later curated-example work. The field-only warm/cold mixed files used by the Quick Start are\nsimpler companions to lessons 5/6, not new physics examples. They are useful when\nyou want E/B/moments without introducing heating/energy interpretation. Secondary numerical and historical examples adaptive_bessel.in and faddeeva_accuracy.in are numerical stress/convergence exercises, not beginner benchmarks. Examine\n  harmonic counts, cap warnings and tensor accuracy as described in Numerics . mixed_scan_warm_reference.in and fully_cold_scan.in are existing serial continuation examples with no failed points in this replay. mixed_scan_cold_reference.in finishes but has a failed written point in current replay. Study NaN/recovery\n  deliberately; use mixed_fields_cold_reference.in for an all-success bounded\n  first scan instead. example_map_par.in is an existing linear-map lesson; examine sampled determinants versus refined\n  candidates and branch slots. It is larger than the Quick Start. example_guess_double.in is a large historical 2D scan; the previous audit interrupted it at its short\n  runtime limit. It is not certified as a small, all-success new-user example. Historical radial/parameter/FPC fixtures retain their specialized limitations.\n  No new curated radial/FPC input or scientific extension is claimed here. Further example work will automate benchmarks and document branch identity.\nExisting inputs are not rewritten by this manual task. Current execution evidence and all linked-input existence\nchecks are recorded in the manual report .","tags":"","url":"page/examples.html"},{"title":"Numerical methods and scientific checks – PLUME","text":"Numerical methods and scientific checks Manual home · Normalization · Limitations This page describes the numerical algorithms. Source constants such as the\nsecant tolerance, derivative ladder and FPC harmonic range are not public\nnamelist fields. Record the source revision and all selectable settings with\neach result. Tensors and the determinant For Fourier convention exp(i k.r-i omega t), B0 along z and k=(kperp,0,kpar),\nPLUME stores the ordinary dimensionless per-population tensor susc(species,row,column)=chi_s . With w=omega/Omega_R,\nkappa=k d_R and a=v_A,R/c, it forms The tensor n n&#94;T has ordinary transpose, not conjugation; complex n is allowed.\nVacuum identity and electromagnetic terms appear exactly once, outside species\nkernels. Tensor component order is xx,xy,xz; yx,yy,yz; zx,zy,zz. The gyrotropic\nresponse has chi_yx=-chi_xy, chi_zx=chi_xz, chi_zy=-chi_yz, without conjugation at complex frequencies. Hermitian symmetry at a real nondissipative point is\na separate physical statement. The API disp and map files use the raw determinant.\nDirect stdout historically multiplies it by solver_vtp&#94;6 for a warm reference,\nwhereas cold-reference direct evaluation reports it raw. This display scaling\ndoes not redefine chi, epsilon or Lambda. Historical comments about multiplying\nexpressions by w&#94;2 do not describe the implemented matrix. A small determinant can result from scaling or cancellation. Check the\nnormalized determinant, matrix/nullvector residual, finiteness and root stability\nunder controlled resolution changes. A small raw determinant alone does not\nestablish frequency accuracy. Kinetic response and normalization adapter The thermal susceptibility follows Stix, Waves in Plasmas , Chapter 10,\nequations 66–73, for the bi-Maxwellian model .\nWarm-reference d_ref inputs are converted to the historical thermal coordinates\nwith the reference anisotropy factor. Cold-reference mixtures use the independent\nauxiliary state, with physical species anisotropies untouched. Physical\nprefactors, Doppler shifts and thermal/Bessel arguments are invariant under that\ncoordinate choice. The adapter reuses the shared kinetic expressions.\nFully cold response bypasses thermal state. Adaptive Bessel harmonics and caching For a warm species define the scaled modified Bessel coefficient , where theta_s is d_ref tauS; the second form applies to a thermal reference. This is\none physical argument, independent of auxiliary scale. Cold species have no\nBessel arrays. Harmonic count N means maximum absolute signed order : -N..N\ncontains 2N+1 terms, with additional adjacent orders stored for combinations. bessel_mode='adaptive' is the default. The selector bounds both the omitted\nordinary weight and the normalized second moment: The selector uses conservative logarithmic bounds from the generating function . For m=N+1 and , It chooses a species-specific N satisfying both bounds at bessel_tol (default\n1e-10), subject to bessel_nmax (default 512). Bounds are evaluated stably in\nlogarithmic form, avoiding subtraction of nearly converged sums from one and\npremature termination for broad distributions.\nFor lambda=0 the selector handles the weights analytically; supported exact\nparallel response uses its analytic harmonic limit. This does not enable the\ncurrently unsupported all-kinetic kperp=0 dispatcher. The tail tolerance does not guarantee total susceptibility or root accuracy. Resonance\namplification, cancellation, Bessel coefficient accuracy and Z accuracy also matter.\nCompare all relevant tensor elements and mode frequency while tightening\nbessel_tol and increasing the cap, especially for broad lambda distributions. If the cap is reached before the bounds meet tolerance, PLUME warns once for the\nunchanged request and continues with the maximum allowed N. The .bessel metadata\nrecords the convergence as unresolved .\nA nonfinite Bessel calculation is numerical failure, distinct from a finite but\nunder-resolved sum. There is no silent physical substitution or damping. The per-species cache tracks physical lambda, request, active N,\nallocation capacity, coefficients, tail bounds and convergence. Frequency,\nparallel wavevector, drift and density alone do not require new physical Bessel\ncoefficients. Perpendicular wavevector, temperature, anisotropy, mass or charge\nchanges that alter lambda do. Exact computed-argument matching avoids unbounded\napproximate reuse; roundoff in forming an equivalent argument can cause a miss,\nnot reuse of incorrect data. Allocation grows as needed without shrinking at\nevery small step. Species-count/order changes cannot reuse the wrong argument. bessel_mode='fixed' uses bessel_nfixed; 'fixed128' requires nfixed=128.\nThe historical fixed-128 compiled arithmetic and its old cache path are retained\nfor exact compatibility. The historical path does not report per-species cache\nstatistics. Changing the auxiliary scale may require recomputation. Independent\nchoices of Z/heating conventions are required for legacy reproduction . FPC distributions use a separate internal fixed nbesmax=15 , not this adaptive\nsusceptibility policy. Neither bessel_tol nor an adaptive root calculation is\nevidence that a velocity-space FPC reconstruction is harmonically converged. Plasma dispersion function For Im zeta>0 the kinetic Z function is The Landau analytic continuation defines it elsewhere. With Faddeeva function , . This mathcal w is not the normalized\nwave frequency w. The signed parallel convention used by the production zet_in(kpar,zeta) interface is Z(zeta) for kpar>0 and -Z(-zeta) for kpar<0.\nIt is not naive lower-half-plane conjugation. z_backend='faddeeva' (default) uses bundled Steven G. Johnson code with a C99\nbridge and Fortran ISO_C_BINDING. The bridge uses upstream evaluation at machine\nprecision; there is no user Z-tolerance setting, and no Python runtime is required.\nIts continued\nfraction/Algorithm-916/Taylor branches are numerical techniques, not changes to\nthe Landau prescription. The historical radius-three approximation error motivated\nreplacement; the audit isolated its susceptibility amplification independently. The Faddeeva backend assigns a result on every call. Invalid/nonfinite arguments,\nunsupported kpar=0 calls, arithmetic failures and nonrepresentable outputs\npropagate invalid status/NaNs through guarded dispersion paths. It does not clamp\nZ, add imaginary frequency or fall back silently to the old algorithm. Some\nlower-half-plane Z values exceed binary64 range; this is distinct from an\nimplementation failure. Subsequent regular evaluations must still succeed.\nFully cold dispersion does not call Z. z_backend='legacy' preserves historical zetout arithmetic/stopping behavior,\nincluding known accuracy and unassigned-result limitations; it is not an accurate\nfallback. The Faddeeva bridge uses c_double/c_double_complex matching the promoted\nFortran real/complex storage. Under these flags, do not assume nrtype::DP is\ninterchangeable with these C kinds. Provenance and license records the upstream mirror commit, checksums, MIT attribution and unchanged\nnumerical sources. No external shared third-party library is required. Drifting cold susceptibility For one species let delta=w-kappa_parallel u, b=kappa_perp u,\ng=mu/Q signed and A=D mu/(Q&#94;2 a&#94;2), using the physical normalization definitions . The species circular and coupling\nterms from Verscharen & Chandran, Section 2.1 are D_H is the Hall coefficient, not the input density ratio D. These are ordinary\nspecies chi terms with no vacuum identity . PLUME uses factored denominators\n(delta-g)(delta+g) and combined transverse formulas to reduce\nsubtractive cancellation. Finite electron mass is retained. At drift zero the\ncross couplings vanish; parallel stationary transverse roots recover n&#94;2=R or L\nfor R=1+sum R_s, L=1+sum L_s. Perpendicular stationary O/X relations are n&#94;2=P\nand n&#94;2=RL/S with the total dielectric coefficients, away from poles. Exact w=0, delta=0 and delta=+/-g are explicitly classified for nonzero density;\nzero density gives zero susceptibility. A full tensor may be invalid at a pole\neven if some individual component has a removable cancellation. No pressure,\nthermal correction, collision floor or artificial damping is added. Root solving, derivatives and numerical checks The production root method is a complex secant iteration, with internal\ntolerance 1e-13, nearby initial fractional offsets 1e-7 and a maximum of\n75 iterations. These constants are not user inputs. See Modes for\nmap minima, deduplication/filter order and continuation. Guarded scan points\nalso check status, finiteness and a determinant divided by the cube of the\nmaximum matrix magnitude against 1e-8. This is a scaling/acceptance safeguard,\nnot a universal frequency error estimate or a new branch tracker. Corrected energy derivatives use centered finite differences with Richardson\nextrapolation and convergence checks on matrices and their field contractions ,\ntogether with analytic cold response derivatives. Their physical meaning and\nresolution limits are on Heating . Failed supplementary\nenergy must not change accepted-mode matrix state or root continuation. For publishable calculations, vary map resolution or supplied guesses, scan\nstep size, Bessel tolerance/cap and backend. Reconstruct Lambda\nfrom species tensors and check field/current identities where relevant. Compare\nforward/reverse traversal with attention to physical mode crossings. Retain\nunresolved cap warnings, invalid status and unavailable diagnostic distinctions\nwhen analyzing or plotting results.","tags":"","url":"page/numerics.html"},{"title":"Limitations and troubleshooting – PLUME","text":"Limitations and troubleshooting Manual home · Capability matrix · Input This page consolidates current restrictions. A warning or accepted process exit\nis not a statement that a calculation is physically supported. A valid dispersion\nroot also does not imply every supplementary diagnostic is available. Geometry and plasma models All-kinetic exact parallel kperp=0 is unsupported , in either normalization.\n  Input/scan preparation warns; the historical kinetic evaluation may return\n  invalid values. Do not use a tiny arbitrary kperp floor to claim the limit. Cold/mixed exact parallel transverse/kinetic limits are supported with nonzero\n  kpar. Their successful tests do not certify an all-kinetic exact-axis dispatcher. Exact perpendicular kpar=0 is supported only for fully cold single-wavevector\n  dispersion. Mixed/warm perpendicular response is deferred. Every wavevector\n  scan, including fully cold scans, rejects kpar=0 at written or hidden points. Ex=1 cannot represent an Ex=0 mode. In particular the cold perpendicular\n  ordinary root may exist but its electromagnetic eigenfunction is unavailable.\n  No alternative field gauge is introduced. Poles, rank degeneracy and off-mode\n  matrices can also invalidate fields. The equilibrium is nonrelativistic, homogeneous and gyrotropic, with parallel\n  equilibrium drifts. Arbitrary distributions, perpendicular equilibrium drifts,\n  relativity and MPI/OpenMP parallelization are not available. Cold-containing input requires collisionless d_ref. Cold anisotropy is ignored;\n  there is no cold pressure or cold kinetic absorption/FPC. Positive tauS, however\n  small, remains kinetic. Model-changing scans through zero are unsupported. Collisions and FPC The all-kinetic collision_type=1 path is a nonconservative neutral-charged Krook\nresponse. It is not a general Coulomb or energy-conserving collision operator.\nThe longitudinal collisional zz correction was independently validated and is\nnot undone by legacy numerical switches. A separate finite-rate xz/yz issue\nremains outside the validated corrected-diagnostic scope. Do not generalize a\npassing zz test to all collisional tensor elements. Corrected heating requires collision_type=0. Explicit legacy all-kinetic\ncollisional diagnostics retain historical behavior, without a new accuracy claim.\nCold/mixed collisions are unsupported. Kn positivity/finiteness and some old\ninputs are incompletely validated; a huge default Kn is not a general collision\nmodel. Read Input and record actual collision_type and Kn. Gyro/Cartesian FPC remains an all-kinetic historical pathway, including its thermal\nsolver/header conventions. There is no mixed-plasma FPC support , even for\nwarm species within a mixture. Susceptibility adaptive harmonics do not make\nFPC's fixed internal harmonic reconstruction adaptive. Distribution moments are\ndebug integrations with substantial numerical limitations, not the validated\nanalytical species moments. Header phase-speed normalization has a reference-\nanisotropy caveat; see FPC appendix . Reader NaN preservation fixes interpretation, not FPC physics, normalization or\nenergy closure. Old species-index labels, header-offset retries and debug-only\nreaders still need scrutiny. Unavailable samples are not physical zeros. The contour/resonance plotter's explicit file reader is not a universal\ncold-reference overlay calculator. Its vthpar_over_ref helper still forms sqrt(mu*theta/beta_R) in d_ref, which is undefined at beta_R=0. Reading a\ncold-reference mode table can therefore work while a reference-thermal\nresonance overlay does not. Use the physical units on Normalization and the\nsupported data readers; no cold-reference overlay extension is claimed here. Diagnostic and workflow boundaries Corrected energy/heating is a real-frequency weak-mode approximation, not exact\n  conserved energy for strongly growing/damped, degenerate or reactive modes.\n  energy_status and applicability differ; formal H may be resolved while Ps is\n  unavailable. Cold real-frequency poles can block energy for a valid complex mode. Map eigen/tensor requests and legacy map heating are ignored with warnings;\n  corrected map heating is rejected. Map file schemas remain determinants and\n  candidates. Use a supported root/diagnostic workflow to obtain moments or heat. Two-dimensional eigen/heating flags come from the second scan; tensor output\n  needs both flags. Flag semantics are not identical across these diagnostics. Mixed/cold scans support wavevectors only. Corrected parameter heating is\n  supported for collisionless, all-kinetic rho_ref serial scans and 2D grids.\n  Other parameter workflows retain historical restrictions; radial heating is\n  legacy only. Scan support does not establish branch identity or heating\n  applicability at every point. Historical logarithmic frequency maps mix exponent origins with ratio\n  increments and discard imaginary signs. They do not reach requested endpoints.\n  Use linear map grids; the exact equations remain unchanged pending a separate compatibility decision. nroot_max controls guesses/continued branch slots, not all .roots rows.\n  Candidate files precede final filters. Sorting by least damping is not the\n  enabled default. A mode label is a continuation slot, not proof of a universal\n  physical branch identity through crossings. Diagnosing a run Symptom Check and next action Indexed namelist cannot be found Use exact lowercase &species_N/&guess_N/&scan_input_N headers, alone on their lines; no trailing header comments; correct consecutive indices Cannot open input/scratch Use a .in suffix, writable input directory and a short path; the historical argument buffer is 100 characters Cannot open output Run from repo root or a deliberately prepared working directory; mkdir -p data/ ; writeOut=false does not suppress opens No .roots after supplied guesses option1/nscan0 refines/prints but does not write map candidate files No eigen/heating file after a map Map diagnostics are ignored/rejected; a flag is not evidence of a supported writer No .energy Require corrected direct diagnostics or a supported scan with heating=true; no map/FPC/legacy energy companion Too few/many roots Inspect finite refined candidates, final filters and nroot_max branch slots; vary map bounds/grid or guesses; .roots is not a final capped list NaN frequency at a scan point Inspect root/status warning, poles and finite matrix; last valid seed is retained on guarded paths; reduce step or reinitialize physically Finite frequency but NaN E/B Ex=0 gauge, unresolved rank/nullvector, off-mode matrix or invalid response; check Lambda E and independent mode polarization Only one species has NaN moments Inspect its status, density and Doppler/cyclotron poles; D=0 gives undefined relative moments; valid other species/fields survive Cold species has NaN Ps/channels Expected unavailability, not zero heat; its H_s may still be valid Warm Ps/channels NaN with finite H Check applicability bits for strong/near-reactive mode, or species/normalizer status; do not force weak energy balance H/fractions unavailable wr=0, real pole, unresolved derivative, cancellation or absent field; no small H floor; retain valid root/moments Bessel cap warning Finite calculation continued with unresolved tails; increase nmax and compare tensors/roots, not just one small coefficient Faddeeva nonrepresentable output Lower-half-plane growth of Z can exceed binary64; distinguish range failure from a physical pole; no silent legacy fallback Different rho/d_ref curves Convert k using sqrt(beta_R alpha_R), vtp and all tau; compare identical physical scan paths, not just identical numbers Missing values appear as zeros in a plot Keep readers' default NaNs; visualization_nan_to_zero is an explicit plot-only opt-in Reader gives plausible wrong diagnostics Select S, eigen, heating, low_n and correct file family; parameters are not heating; do not use debug .eigen as .modeN Ambiguous serial contour file Call reader API with scan_index=N; CLI currently has no selector; 2D schema uses scan2 flags Ragged low_n=false legacy file Orphan Ew record is historical; use explicit handling or supported rectangular/corrected companion; never treat Ew as energy Extra old mode files or mismatched companion counts Use a new outputName/isolated directory; complete the run; companions and buffered modes are not atomic under interruption","tags":"","url":"page/limitations.html"},{"title":"Historical reproduction and provenance – PLUME","text":"Historical reproduction and provenance Manual home · Numerical methods · Heating There are four different meanings of “legacy.” Record which one you need.\nNone of the input switches selects the others automatically. Choice Setting What is preserved Z evaluator z_backend='legacy' Historical zetout numerical arithmetic/stopping/failure limitations Harmonic sum bessel_mode='fixed128' , bessel_nfixed=128 Historical maximum absolute order 128, i.e. -128..128, and original fixed-context arithmetic Heating convention heating_convention='legacy' Historical all-kinetic normalizer, derivative, harmonic/scratch and output behavior Physical/source history A specific Git commit, compiler and flags The equations, dispatch, bug fixes, formats and build rules in that revision Current defaults are Faddeeva/adaptive/corrected. Old files omitting the\nselectors use current defaults , even though omitted normalization still\ndefaults to rho_ref. Selecting fixed128 alone does not reproduce historical Z\nor heating. A legacy file should state all needed controls explicitly: & params normalization = 'rho_ref' z_backend = 'legacy' bessel_mode = 'fixed128' bessel_nfixed = 128 heating_convention = 'legacy' ! Supply the remaining physical/workflow inputs as in the working file below. / That snippet is a selector fragment, not a complete runnable plasma. The existing heating_legacy.in is complete and runnable: make -j2\nmkdir -p data/example\n./plume.e inputs/example/heating_legacy.in It produces the old all-kinetic mode heating blocks; do not expect corrected\nvacuum-electric energy or a newly written .energy companion. Legacy cold/mixed\nheating is unavailable. The old Ew slot is not a trustworthy wave energy.\nLegacy Z intentionally retains approximation/failure defects, rather than being\nan accurate fallback for an invalid modern evaluation. What numerical selectors do not undo These switches do not revert the separately corrected collisional zz physics,\nnew validation/warnings, new model dispatch, Python reader corrections, native\ninput definitions, or historical logarithmic-map coordinates. They do not\nrestore arbitrary old file formats or turn off all later behavior. For exact\nold physics or byte-level reproduction of a different release, use its source\nrevision and compatible toolchain in a separate checkout. Do not overwrite a\nworking research tree to emulate an older calculation. The existing exact tests pin pre-normalization commit c6c7616e1fc58eb6118e1f9423a3f0df6d52ef20 for two collisionless numerical\nfixtures, and 8331925c7953dcc68f29f14c1d7b78dd9198bd5a for kinetic scan/field\ncompatibility. Those are specific verified comparisons with explicit settings,\nnot a promise of universal byte identity for every input/compiler/platform.","tags":"","url":"page/reproducibility.html"},{"title":"Developer APIs, verification and historical reports – PLUME","text":"Developer APIs, verification and historical reports Manual home · Numerics · Input contract The scientific user entry points are the manual and input workflows, not a list\nof generic internal procedures. FORD-generated API documentation remains useful\nfor developers tracing exact signatures and source comments. It is not an\nalternative authoritative definition when historical comments conflict with\ncurrent equations, output writers or tests. Dependency map plume program -> functions : parse indexed namelists and validate -> map_search / refine_guess / scan loops -> disp : composition / normalization dispatch -> kinetic or auxiliary kinetic response -> cold susceptibility -> species susc + total lam + determinant -> electromagnetic fields and species moments -> corrected local heating / energy snapshots -> companion writer -> mode / tensor writers Species chi uses ordinary dimensionless susceptibility; lambda includes vacuum\nand electromagnetic terms once. solver_* are derived thermal coordinates,\nnot authoritative physical inputs. Cold-reference and fully cold dispatch must\nnot construct a physical zero-temperature gyroradius. Heating derivatives must\nnot leave global state at the real or perturbed frequency rather than the\naccepted complex mode. API entry points FORD links below resolve in a built current-source site. They retain the existing\nmodules/procedures rather than introducing a second documentation API. Interface Purpose disprels / disp Public production determinant callback, ordinary species susc and total lam functions / read_in_params Input parser and indexed-group setup vars Physical species/scanner types, input and derived state calc_chi_cold Explicit single-species cold response, native inertial coordinates, status codes calc_warm_auxiliary Warm response in explicit auxiliary coordinates, physical inputs read-only calc_em_eigen / calc_em_fields Existing Ex=1 field method with explicit physical normalization calc_cold_moments / calc_kinetic_moments Explicit species fluid or susceptibility-current moment kernels calculate_heating / write_energy_record State-isolated corrected diagnostics and versioned companion writer zet_in Production-facing signed-k Z interface, selectable backend rtsec Existing secant method; not a replacement root algorithm fpc Historical all-kinetic FPC distribution/debug routines The new numerical modules follow compiler-promoted production real/complex\nkinds and strict IEEE behavior where required. Do not assume nrtype::DP is\nbinary64 under every promoted build. The Faddeeva C ABI explicitly uses the\nISO C double-complex interface. Shared kinetic includes avoid independently\nedited copies of the equations; fixed128 compiler-sensitive arithmetic is\nprotected by exact tests. Verification make -j2 MPLCONFIGDIR = /tmp/plume-mpl python3 -m unittest discover -s tests -p 'test_*.py' -v Tests include independent cold fluid/current solutions, NHDS/Stix tensor\nreferences, SciPy/Faddeeva and optional arbitrary precision, componentwise\nsusceptibility checks, roots, field/nullspace/Faraday checks, energy derivatives,\nreader markers, wrapper roundtrips and checked Fortran builds. A passing suite\nis evidence for those cases, not universal validation of collisional/FPC or\nstrong-mode interpretations. Exact expected data should not be edited to hide\nnumerical changes. Historical reproduction identifies the\npinned comparisons and settings. Local documentation build The current manual sources are the Markdown files in docs/ , with docs/index.md as the homepage and images in docs/assets/ . The GitHub overview\nremains in the root README.md . From the repository root, run: ./scripts/build_docs.sh\nxdg-open docs_out/page/index.html The script requires Python 3, FORD and Graphviz. It stages the complete manual\nin a temporary directory, converts local Markdown links without changing the\nsources, and generates API documentation from the current Fortran sources.\nHistorical reports remain in tests/ and are linked through the evidence index\nbelow rather than listed in primary navigation. Before replacing docs_out/ , the builder checks all manual pages, local links,\nfragments, images, parameter anchors, rendered tables, math markup and linked API\ndestinations. It preserves the previous output in a uniquely named backup.\nDetailed validation results and separate generated API link issues are recorded\nin docs_out/validation.json ; FORD warnings are recorded in docs_out/build.log .\nThe checker can also be run independently: python3 tests/check_user_manual.py --site docs_out The documentation CI workflow uses the same builder and retains its existing\npublication triggers. Running the local script does not deploy a website or\nfetch the optional ADS citing-paper list. External references are not certified\nby local link checks, and MathJax uses an external CDN. Existing FORD interface\nanchor defects and source-comment parsing warnings can remain even when manual\nvalidation passes; inspect the generated reports rather than treating a\nsuccessful build as scientific validation. Historical development evidence The following reports describe the state at their development milestone .\nStatements such as “heating is deferred” or “warm species with a cold reference\nare rejected” may be superseded. Use Models , Heating and Output for the current supported contract. The reports retain\nscientific derivations, independent benchmarks and implementation rationale. Topic Historical report / evidence Dual normalization Original implementation audit Cold kernel convention Cold susceptibility design Mixed dispersion, warm reference Mixed dispersion report Fully cold dispersion Fully cold report Auxiliary scale mathematics Auxiliary audit Cold-reference mixed production Cold-reference report Adaptive harmonics/cache Bessel report Z accuracy audit Plasma dispersion audit Faddeeva integration Faddeeva report Collisional zz Longitudinal correction ; not general collisional validation Mixed scans Scan report Electromagnetic fields Field report Species moments Cold-fluid/kinetic moments Heating audit and correction Audit , corrected implementation Documentation/output inventory Stage 5A Targeted reader/workflow fixes Stage 5B.0 Companion audit JSON files remain alongside these reports. None of their frozen\nhistorical measurements is rewritten to match current documentation or tests.","tags":"","url":"page/development.html"},{"title":"JET-PLUME: historical field-particle correlations – PLUME","text":"JET-PLUME: historical field-particle correlations Manual home · Input reference · Output readers JET-PLUME (Judging Energy Transfer in a Plasma in a Linear Uniform Magnetized\nEnvironment) reconstructs linear velocity-space distribution perturbations and\nfield-particle correlation (FPC) signatures. It is an all-kinetic historical\npathway , separate from corrected species Ps and wave energy. Cold/mixed FPC is\nnot implemented, including warm populations in a mixture. New density/velocity\nmoments and corrected heating do not enable that future functionality. Original JET-PLUME contributions are credited to Collin Brown, Gregory Howes,\nKristopher Klein and Jason TenBarge. The PLUME paper and existing scientific\nreferences provide context. Workflows and coordinates option=6 invokes compute_fpc_gyro : distributions/correlations are reduced over\nvelocity gyrophase to a (vperp,vpar) grid. option=7 invokes compute_fpc_cart :\nCartesian velocity-space reconstruction, three projected planes, and 3D/debug\noutputs. Earlier prose reversing these routines was incorrect. In both cases\nuse_map selects map initialization versus supplied guesses refined by the solver;\nthere is not an independent mixed/cold FPC root solver. The background field is z, k lies in x-z, x is perpendicular in the wave/field\nplane, y is its second perpendicular direction, and z is parallel. Grid velocities\nuse the historical species parallel thermal-speed coordinates, not the\nspecies-moment output normalization. FPC grid construction/header values still\nuse the derived rho_ref solver convention for d_ref inputs. Add &fpc to an otherwise valid all-kinetic input. This is a parameter fragment,\nnot a standalone runnable plasma: & fpc vperpmin = 0 , vperpmax = . 5 , vparmin =- . 5 , vparmax = . 5 vxmin =- . 5 , vxmax = . 5 , vymin =- . 5 , vymax = . 5 , vzmin =- . 5 , vzmax = . 5 delv = . 5 , elecdircontribution = 0 / Those bounds/spacing are exercised by the existing FPC normalization test: PYTHONPATH = tests MPLCONFIGDIR = /tmp/plume-mpl python3 -m unittest \\ test_normalization.NormalizationTests.test_fpc_diagnostics -v A curated standalone public FPC lesson is still outstanding. The existing example notebook shows the optional wrapper's historical workflow; review mode/schema assumptions\nbefore reuse. This stage does not create or rewrite input examples. Use positive delv and integer-compatible bounds. The writer derives integer grid\nindices; noninteger-multiple bounds warn and may cause off-by-one reader behavior.\nGyro bounds matter for option6; Cartesian bounds for option7. delv is shared across\ndirections. elecdircontribution is a debug forcing selector: 1 keeps Ex, 2 Ey,\n3 Ez, and 0/other leaves forcing unchanged. It is not a heating-channel selector. Meaning and normalization caveats The distribution files contain real/imaginary parts of the internally normalized\nlinear Fourier coefficient f_s1, not an independent physical measured distribution.\nFor Cartesian internal thermal-coordinate velocity vhat_i and distribution\ncoefficient fhat_1, the literal implemented weight is Here Q_s is the code's input charge ratio q_R/q_s, E_i is the Ex=1 field\ncomponent, and vhat/fhat are the FPC routine's internal thermal variables. This\nstates the implemented nondimensional weight, not a new dimensional-power\nnormalization. Projection integrates the omitted coordinate with the grid\nweights; gyro reduction includes the cylindrical Jacobian/gyrophase weights\nand a special near-zero radial cell treatment. There are existing finite-grid,\nharmonic and normalization limitations. Do not equate these raw weights or their\ndebug integrals with corrected Ps or entropy production without a separate\nnormalization/closure analysis. A universal dimensional conversion for these\nhistorical FPC outputs is not newly validated by this manual. Headers labeled omega.r/omega.i literally contain real/imaginary parts of\nw sqrt(beta_R)/Kpar, where w=omega/Omega_R and Kpar=kpar rho_R in solver\ncoordinates. Since rho_R/d_R=sqrt(beta_R alpha_R), this equals , not the physical phase speed in\nv_A,R units when alpha_R differs from one. Old stdout/plot labels claiming an\nunqualified Alfvén-normalized phase speed need that caveat. A future FPC audit\nmust address the full header/grid/normalization contract before broader use. FPC reconstruction has fixed internal harmonic range nbesmax=15; adaptive\nsusceptibility Bessels do not change it. Numerical distribution moments/debug\nintegrals are not the validated analytic current/continuity species moments.\nTheir quadrature can be inaccurate. No mixed FPC or new collisional accuracy\nclaim is made here. Files and array orientation One indexed set is produced per population and root, with species in the original\ninput order, generally .specieSS.modeMM . Old readers use index-based electron\nlabels and offset recovery; those heuristics are not arbitrary-mixture validation. Gyro files: option6 Quantity suffix Contents .cpar Parallel-field correlation .cperp Perpendicular-field correlation .df1gyro.real Real reduced perturbed distribution coefficient .df1gyro.imag Imaginary reduced perturbed distribution coefficient Each has eight physical/thermal header labels/values, five grid labels/values,\na separator, then a matrix. vperp advances by row, vpar by column; the first\nentry corresponds to the minimum grid values. The matrix is a gyro-reduced\nvelocity-space object, not a projected Cartesian map mislabeled as gyro. Cartesian files: option7 Quantity suffix Contents .cparcart Ez correlation, projected into three planes .cperp1 Ex correlation, projected into three planes .cperp2 Ey correlation, projected into three planes .dfs.real, .dfs.imag Real/imaginary distribution projected into those planes .dfs3D.real, .dfs3D.imag Real/imaginary full 3D distribution blocks Projection files contain (x,y), (x,z), (z,y) array layouts separated by --- .\nThe first has rows x / columns y; second rows x / columns z; third rows z /\ncolumns y. This last historical orientation assumes compatible y/z grids;\nit is not a reason to transpose every loaded diagnostic blindly. The old .df1.real/.imag naming in prose does not match the current .dfs writer. Three-dimensional blocks use reader storage (z,y,x), one writer line per (x,y)\ncontaining z values. They have a separate block contract and must not be passed\nto the projected-file parser. Source/header-specific debug .eigen / .mom files\nare described on Output ; they have no ordinary\nscan species-parameter suffix and are unsuitable as a general scientific reader. Some FPC writers already zero extremely small values and saturate large ones\n(typical thresholds 9.999e-99 and 9.999e99). Reader corrections cannot reconstruct\nvalues lost by that historical formatting. A zero in such a file is not proof of\nan exactly vanishing physical correlation; inspect its source/precision regime.\nNo clipping thresholds or numerical FPC calculation are changed by this manual. Reading and missing values Use loadlinfpccepar , loadlinfpcceperp , loadlinfpcgyro_dist for gyro; loadlinfpccart / loadlinfpccart_dist for projections; loadlinfpc3d / loadlinfpc3d_dist for full Cartesian distribution blocks.\nReaders preserve NaNs, infinities, genuine finite zeros, signs and shape by\ndefault. visualization_nan_to_zero=True explicitly replaces only NaNs for\nplots; it is not a physical value or analysis correction. The 3D reader no\nlonger clips representable finite samples itself, though old writers may already\nhave done so. Distinguish a missing diagnostic from a physical calculated zero.","tags":"","url":"page/README-JETPLUME.html"}]}